{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"article\": 1,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia vs. Broadcom: Which AI Chip Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-06\",\n  \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) and Broadcom Inc. (NASDAQ: AVGO ) offer two different claims on the AI computing budget. Nvidia sells a broad GPU platform supported by software and integrated systems. Broadcom helps customers build custom accelerators and supplies networking alongside a substantial software business. An investor choosing between them needs to decide which company can retain more cash as customers seek cheaper computing.\\nAt the October 5 close, their trailing cash-flow valuations were close enough that the business differences matter more than a small discount. My preference is Nvidia, provided its software advantage continues to protect margins and its infrastructure guarantees do not absorb that benefit. Broadcom becomes more compelling if custom chips gain share while financing support at both companies stays contained.\\nThe custom-chip choice extends beyond these two stocks. Our Broadcom-versus-Marvell comparison examines how much investors are paying for current cash generation versus later customer ramps .\\nNvidia's margin is the advantage custom chips must erode\\nNvidia's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year. Its 75% GAAP gross margin indicates that buyers were still paying well above the company's production costs. CUDA's libraries, development tools and compatibility with established applications help explain the attraction: changing hardware can also require engineering work and performance validation. That creates a cost of switching beyond the processor's purchase price.\\nThe bullish case is that customers keep valuing that flexibility as models and workloads change. Nvidia can earn from a platform usable across many buyers rather than depend on the success of one customer's chip design. The bearish case is that large customers eventually find enough predictable work to justify specialized alternatives. Custom silicon need not replace every GPU to limit Nvidia's pricing power on the workloads where substitution is economical.\\nDeployment constraints can also delay demand even when the computing opportunity remains attractive. Our analysis of Nvidia's flexible AI factory asks which operating test extra funding alone cannot solve . Power availability and useful computing output belong in the demand assessment alongside a customer's announced budget.\\nHistorical ownership gives Nvidia broader long-side participation: Insider Monkey's hedge fund database recorded 285 holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management held 90,935,947 shares after increasing its position about 3%. Those positions predate the August results. At the September 15 settlement, 294,225,803 shares were sold short, about 1.27% of float, giving limited support for a thesis built around crowded short positioning.\\nBroadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2. AI semiconductor revenue rose 221% to $16.7 billion. That is the strongest operating argument for choosing Broadcom: customers pursuing custom computing are already producing substantial sales. Its latest quarter also generated $13.7 billion of free cash flow, equal to 46% of revenue.\\nCustom designs can build durable engineering relationships, while networking gives Broadcom another way to earn as clusters expand. Infrastructure software supplied $8.75 billion of quarterly revenue. It offers a separate profit pool, although license revenue means the segment's sales are not uniformly recurring subscriptions. The investment case depends on retained margins and collections as much as the announced chip opportunity.\\nConcentration makes those economics more sensitive to a few buyers. Broadcom's five largest end customers accounted for 55% of quarterly revenue. A large program can support efficient development, but a buyer with alternatives can bargain for a greater share of the savings. Our dividend analysis tests how much protection today's cash coverage provides when those customers gain negotiating power .\\nThe financing risk also deserves a place in the comparison. Broadcom disclosed a maximum potential liability of about $29 billion under a five-year customer-lease backstop upon deployment of all relevant AI racks. No amounts had been paid, and the backstop's fair value was immaterial. For scale, that contingent maximum equals nearly three-quarters of its $39.4 billion annual trailing free cash flow, although the potential obligations extend across several years. A customer default and weaker equipment resale values could make financing support costly just when demand deteriorates.\\nBroadcom's hedge-fund-holder count fell to 170 in Q2 2026 from 173 in Q1. Fisher Asset Management increased its share position about 3% to 15,131,673. The manager increased both holdings while the aggregate holder counts moved differently. These disclosures describe historical exposure; the operating and valuation comparison must supply the investment judgment.\\nAt closing prices of $239.09 for Nvidia and $362.51 for Broadcom on October 5, the stocks traded at about 45.6 and 44.0 times trailing free cash flow, respectively. Both measures use equity value divided by trailing operating cash flow less capital expenditure, through July 26 for Nvidia and August 2 for Broadcom. Nvidia's premium was only about 4%. Broadcom's rapid AI growth could justify that small valuation advantage. Its customer concentration qualifies the bargain, while contingent financing exposure requires scrutiny at both companies.\\nThe cash measure also has limits. It adds back stock compensation, includes interest and can fluctuate with working capital. It does not deduct every acquisition or strategic investment. Nvidia's capital-expenditure figure includes intangible-asset purchases but excludes equipment and intangible-asset principal payments, unlike the company's own free-cash-flow definition.\\nNvidia's latest quarterly filing also disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. Exposure generally begins as each lease commences, with the first data centers expected to be placed in service in fiscal 2029. Payments depend on specified tenant defaults; guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. The cap is not current debt or an expected loss. Broadcom's five-year rack backstop and Nvidia's longer infrastructure guarantees have different assets, remedies and timing; their headline maximums cannot establish which stock has the safer financing profile.\\nSupply obligations add another demand risk. Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost. Broadcom disclosed $126.8 billion of unconditional purchase commitments, mainly inventory. Neither total is an immediate cash bill, but slower customer spending could leave commitments harder to absorb.\\nAn illustrative multiple contraction shows the hurdle facing both stocks. If a 45-times cash-flow valuation fell to 30 times, cash flow would need to rise 50% just to preserve the same equity value, assuming no change in share count. Over two years, that requires growth of about 22.5% annually before providing capital appreciation. This is a sensitivity, not a forecast or a fair-value estimate.\\nNvidia seems preferable at this relatively small premium because its broad software platform gives it a stronger route to retaining pricing across changing workloads. That judgment would weaken if custom chips took enough economically attractive work to reduce Nvidia's margins while Broadcom sustained superior cash-flow growth, or if Nvidia's guarantee payments consumed the cash benefit of its platform. Broadcom would then offer the better exposure, provided its own financing commitments remained manageable. For now, the decisive evidence is cash growth after the costs of winning and funding demand, with continued margin strength making Nvidia my preferred stock.\\nWhile we acknowledge the potential of NVDA and AVGO as investments, we believe certain other AI stocks offer greater upside potential and carry less downside risk. If you're looking for an extremely undervalued AI stock that also stands to benefit significantly from Trump-era tariffs and the onshoring trend, see our free report on the best short-term AI stock .\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's August 26 report showed revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, 2026, up 106% year over year, at a 75% GAAP gross margin, according to the article's account of the results.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's latest quarterly filing disclosed guarantees signed in August capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy; exposure generally begins as each lease commences, the first data centers are expected to be placed in service in fiscal 2029, payments depend on specified tenant defaults, and guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. The article notes the cap is not current debt or an expected loss.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost, per its latest disclosure cited in the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Broadcom reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2, 2026, with AI semiconductor revenue up 221% to $16.7 billion, per its September 2 results.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"How large is Nvidia's guarantee exposure supporting the OpenAI affiliate's SB Energy infrastructure, and will any guarantee payments crystallize?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of Nvidia's guarantees (currently capped at $105 billion) reported at its next annual report, and whether any payments are made under them.\",\n      \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027), since the guarantees were already signed in August 2026 and are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"How large are Nvidia's supply and capacity commitments, and how much of that total is adjustable or cancelable?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of Nvidia's supply and capacity commitments (currently $279 billion) and their cancelability reported at its next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (FY2027 Form 10-K, expected early 2027), since the commitments are already undertaken and are tracked by their amount at the next annual report.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's August 26 report showed quarterly revenue of $96.2 billion for the quarter ended July 26, up 106% year over year, with a 75% GAAP gross margin.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's latest quarterly filing disclosed guarantees signed in August, capped at $105 billion, supporting an OpenAI affiliate's infrastructure with SB Energy. Exposure begins as each lease commences, with the first data centers expected to be placed in service in fiscal 2029; payments depend on specified tenant defaults, guaranteed amounts decline over 20-year leases and cover defined portions of lease and power payments. The cap is not current debt or an expected loss.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments across future fiscal years, some adjustable or cancelable at a cost.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Broadcom's September 2 results reported $29.6 billion of quarterly revenue, up 86%, for the period ended August 2, with AI semiconductor revenue up 221% to $16.7 billion and $13.7 billion of free cash flow equal to 46% of revenue.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 2,\n  \"title\": \"3 Overlooked Growth Stocks Powering Every AI Chip Nvidia and AMD Build\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-06\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) are seeing booming demand for graphics processing units (GPUs) . The consensus estimate has both companies growing revenue roughly 90% and 45%, respectively, this year. But you don't have to choose between winners and losers in this GPU rivalry.\\nEvery chip still has to be designed, printed, and paired with fast memory before it can train or run an AI model. ASML ( ASML +1.86% ) , Synopsys ( SNPS +6.03% ) , and Micron Technology ( MU +4.25% ) provide these essential steps in the chipmaking process, offering investors another way to ride the growing demand across the chip industry.\\nASML supplies the extreme ultraviolet lithography systems that print circuit patterns on chips. Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing builds Nvidia and AMD GPUs, and it buys that equipment from ASML. Without those tools, the GPUs do not get made.\\nManagement sees surging demand for AI chips, prompting customers to add manufacturing capacity. Analysts expect full-year 2026 sales to grow 32% over 2025, then another 27% in 2027. Order momentum was \\\"extremely strong\\\" in the first half of 2026, driving backlog growth across a broad mix of customers.\\nThe shares trade at a price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio of 57 as of Oct. 1, 2026. That valuation leaves downside risk if growth stalls. However, CEO Christophe Fouquet said on the second-quarter earnings call that the company has demand visibility extending several years.\\nIn 2027 estimates, though, the multiple falls to 30 times earnings. Analysts expect 31% annualized earnings growth, a P/E-to-growth (PEG) ratio of about 1.0. That valuation leaves room for upside as the semiconductor industry continues to grow.\\nSynopsys is a leading supplier of electronic design automation (EDA), the software Nvidia and AMD use to design chips before being sent to TSMC. Growth is accelerating as AI and high-performance compute customers run more designs.\\nRevenue surged 42% year over year to nearly $2.5 billion in the fiscal third quarter of 2026. It converts that revenue into robust profits, earning an adjusted operating margin of 41.6%. A $10.9 billion backlog indicates more growth ahead.\\nAMD designed its Instinct MI4155X AI GPU with Synopsys' 3DIC Compiler. Synopsys offers innovation that sets it apart in a competitive industry. Its Multiphysics Fusion is the only product that integrates thermal analysis into the design flow, and Nvidia, Cisco Systems , MediaTek, and Samsung Foundry have validated it.\\nRevenue has grown steadily over the past 10 years, including during the 2022 chip industry downturn. Its consistent revenue growth reflects recurring software licenses that smooth out its revenue generation. The stock still looks like a solid buy, trading at a forward P/E of 28 on 2027 estimates, against expected annualized earnings growth of 22%.\\nMicron supplies DRAM and NAND flash memory for high-speed data retrieval and storage in AI data centers. Advanced versions are co-packaged with Nvidia and AMD GPUs, so a stronger GPU cycle pulls memory with it. Micron is also the only U.S.-based supplier of high bandwidth memory, which makes it a natural partner for both.\\nFiscal 2026 revenue hit a record $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year. Gross margin expanded 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a supply shortage lifted memory prices and profits.\\nManagement expects higher gross margins in fiscal 2027, though only a moderate rise in prices. Selling prices are the key risk. Long-term custom agreements account for over 35% of revenue through 2030, leaving a large share exposed to pricing swings.\\nManagement still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year. Micron is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E memory for next-generation GPUs.\\nOverbuilding supply to meet demand could eventually put pressure on memory prices. The prospect of another downturn in selling prices and earnings is why the market is pricing the stock at a conservative 7 times forward earnings. Still, there could be substantial upside if Micron can sign more customer agreements, thereby extending visibility into future revenue.\\nBy John Ballard – Oct 6, 2026 at 8:48AM EST\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will high-bandwidth memory (HBM/DRAM) contract prices rise in calendar 2027 — the input price several AI-chip makers, Nvidia included, depend on?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The direction and size of HBM/DRAM contract price changes for calendar 2027 (e.g., TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract price data and the pricing disclosed by Micron, SK Hynix and Samsung), versus Micron's stated expectation of only a moderate price rise in its fiscal 2027 and the shortage-driven price and margin expansion it reported for fiscal 2026.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 (next calendar year after this 6 October 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a shared quantity; checkable in contract-price releases and memory makers' quarterly reports through 2027.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Consensus estimates cited by the article have Nvidia growing revenue roughly 90% in 2026 and AMD about 45% ('this year'), per a 6 October 2026 article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"ASML management says surging AI-chip demand is prompting its customers to add manufacturing capacity, and that order momentum was 'extremely strong' in the first half of 2026, driving backlog growth across a broad mix of customers. ASML supplies the lithography equipment that TSMC, which builds Nvidia's GPUs, buys.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology reported fiscal 2026 revenue of a record $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year, with gross margin expanding 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a supply shortage lifted memory prices and profits.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology management guided fiscal 2027 to higher gross margins with only a moderate rise in selling prices, and still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology says it is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E memory for next-generation GPUs.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The Motley Fool's John Ballard reports the consensus estimate has Nvidia's revenue growing roughly 90% and AMD's revenue roughly 45% this year (2026).\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Memory supplier Micron Technology reported record fiscal 2026 revenue of $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year, with gross margin expanding 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a memory supply shortage lifted prices and profits.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology management said it expects higher gross margins in fiscal 2027 on only a moderate rise in selling prices, and still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology disclosed that long-term custom agreements account for over 35% of its revenue through 2030, leaving the remaining share of its memory revenue exposed to pricing swings.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Micron Technology is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E high-bandwidth memory for Nvidia's next-generation GPUs, according to the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 3,\n  \"title\": \"Prediction: $500 a Month Invested in Nvidia Stock Will Be Worth This Much by Year-end 2030\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-06\",\n  \"text\": \"Artificial intelligence (AI) has created some enormous multibaggers for investors, but none are quite on the same level as Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) . When ChatGPT helped ignite the generative AI boom in late 2022, Nvidia was worth less than $400 billion. Today, the chipmaker's market capitalization has ballooned to roughly $5.6 trillion -- making it the most valuable company in the world .\\nThis parabolic ascent shows how Nvidia evolved from a generic semiconductor company known for gaming GPUs into the undisputed king of AI infrastructure. Let's assess what might happen if you invested $500 in Nvidia every month from now through the end of 2030. Spoiler alert: Big gains could be in store.\\nHow big can Nvidia get?\\nBefore crunching any numbers, I need to come up with a baseline of where Nvidia might be headed over the next several years. The company generated $96.2 billion in revenue during its most recent quarter, representing a staggering 106% increase from the prior year. Even more impressive, Nvidia's data center business generated $89 billion, growing 117% year over year.\\nThe next chapter is already quietly unfolding. Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production. The company says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous generation Grace Blackwell architecture. This matters because AI spending is moving beyond simply training large language models (LLMs). Reasoning models, AI agents , robotics, and other inference workloads require enormous amounts of continuous computing power.\\nMeanwhile, infrastructure commitments from big tech are getting larger. The big five hyperscalers -- Amazon , Alphabet , Microsoft , Meta Platforms , and Oracle -- plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures (capex) in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027. Meanwhile, Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade.\\nNvidia is also expanding how it can monetize data centers. The company isn't merely selling GPUs anymore. AI factories now combine GPUs with other solutions from Nvidia, including CPUs, NVLink, networking, BlueField data processing units, storage infrastructure, and software platforms like CUDA .\\nI think complementing the legacy GPU business with adjacent solutions should help Nvidia expand its market share in the data center realm. Against this backdrop, I think the company can rise from a $5.6 trillion market value today to $15 trillion by 2030.\\nWhat investing $500 a month in Nvidia looks like\\nAn investor putting $500 into Nvidia every month would invest $6,000 over the course of a full year. Assuming the strategy begins in October 2026 and continues through December 2030, the contributions would total $25,500.\\nUnfortunately, calculating the ending value isn't as simple as multiplying $25,500 by Nvidia's expected return. The reason is because you will be using a dollar-cost averaging strategy over the lifetime of the investment. This means the initial $500 invested in October 2026 has more than four years to appreciate, while a $500 investment made near the end of 2030 has almost no time to compound.\\nIf I assume Nvidia's market capitalization rises gradually from $5.6 trillion to $15 trillion by the end of 2030, that works out to a 163% cumulative increase, or roughly 26% annualized or 1.9% per month when compounded. While this is a bit aggressive, I think it is also a reasonable upside scenario considering it's nowhere near the returns Nvidia generated during earlier stages of the AI boom.\\nIf I run the calculated return across all 51 monthly investments, the $25,500 investment could finish 2030 worth approximately $43,400. That represents roughly $17,900 in investment gains, or 70% upside.\\nObviously, no one knows what Nvidia will be worth four years from now. If the company reaches a $10 trillion valuation by the end of 2030, the same $500 per month strategy would grow to roughly $34,400. If the AI infrastructure supercycle sends Nvidia all the way to $20 trillion, the investment could approach $51,600 -- nearly a 100% gain.\\nThe first takeaway here is that Nvidia has enough structural tailwinds to support meaningful share price appreciation over the next several years. The second takeaway is to understand is that my estimates assume Nvidia's share price generally follows a smooth, compounded path toward the 2030 target.\\nIn reality, the stock's movement will almost certainly be messier. This means the price could rise or fall significantly from month to month. That's actually the entire point of dollar-cost averaging: rather than trying to time the market and predict exactly when Nvidia's stock will rise or fall, investors consistently put the same amount of money to work. For someone investing consistently every month, patience will ultimately matter just as much as finding the right opportunities.\\nBy Adam Spatacco – Oct 6, 2026 at 12:00PM EST\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform — the data center product generation that carries the company's revenue — reach volume customer shipments, confirmed in reported results, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Vera Rubin-based systems shipping in volume and contributing to reported revenue, as confirmed by Nvidia.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date: Nvidia's first quarterly results covering the quarter after the Oct 6, 2026 article (the item reports the platform moving into full production but gives no shipment date, so the check date is the next quarterly report following the item).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest hyperscalers (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle) spend on capital expenditures in aggregate in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Combined calendar-2026 capital expenditures of the big five hyperscalers versus the approximately $800 billion figure cited in the item.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026; settled by the five companies' calendar-2026 full-year results (reported in early 2027), with quarterly capex disclosures as interim checks.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue in its most recent quarter, up 106% year over year, including $89 billion from its data center business, up 117% year over year, as cited by Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production, according to the article, which identifies it as the successor generation to the company's Grace Blackwell architecture.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fool.com's Adam Spatacco states that the big five hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures in calendar 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade, according to Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue of $96.2 billion in its most recent quarter, a 106% increase from the prior year, with its data center business generating $89 billion, up 117% year over year, as cited by Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production, and NVIDIA says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous-generation Grace Blackwell architecture, according to Fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The big five hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027, Fool.com reports.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade, according to Fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 4,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Forecast: Rubin Ramp and $108 Billion Q3 Outlook Keep AI Growth Accelerating\",\n  \"publisher\": \"tradingkey.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-06\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock Forecast: Rubin Ramp and $108 Billion Q3 Outlook Keep AI Growth Accelerating\\nNvidia achieved record financial performance in October 2026, driven by fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubling year-over-year to $96.2 billion, robust Data Center growth, and strong gross margins of 75.0%. Management projects Q3 revenue of approximately $108 billion, supported by the scaling of Blackwell Ultra and the full production ramp of Vera Rubin. Strategic initiatives include a $150 billion share buyback authorization and a proposed acquisition of Hugging Face. Key operational risks involve data center power constraints, customer financing challenges, and regulatory scrutiny. Technically, the stock remains bullish above the $233.17 support level, targeting a breakout past $242.15.\\nNvidia (NVDA) continues to reach record highs in October 2026. On October 5th, NVDA closed at $238.90, up 2.12% with an intraday high of $240.10. As a result, the market capitalization of NVDAis ~5.77T making it the most valuable publicly traded company.\\nWhile sentiment around NVDA has been quite bullish for quite some time, I do think the debate is beginning to shift. Investors are beginning to discuss the constraints that limit Nvidia’s growth, as opposed to discussing whether customers want NVDA’s accelerators. Blackwell Ultra is ramping, Vera Rubin is in full production, and data center revenue has more than doubled. The bigger constraint is whether customers can secure adequate power and financing, and data center space to deploy Nvidia’s accelerators.\\nNvidia Expands Buyback Capacity by $150 Billion\\nOn September 28, Nvidia disclosed that the board increased the company’s authorization for share repurchases by an additional $150 billion. Based on the SEC filing, “the repurchase of NVDA common stock shall occur from time to time in open market purchases, privately negotiated transactions or otherwise in compliance with all applicable securities laws and regulations”.\\nNvidia increased its share buyback authorization by $150 billion on September 28, the largest share repurchase authorization increase in history. Management currently expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028, and currently has about $235 billion remaining under the authorization.\\nThe buyback program demonstrates management’s confidence in the financial markets and Nvidia’s future cash generation potential to consistently execute its strategic imperatives and generate strong cash flow.\\nThe buyback program also creates confidence in management’s financial stewardship in that they expect to maintain product leadership and fund significant share repurchases going forward.\\nHugging Face Deal Expands Nvidia Beyond Hardware\\nOn September 3, 2026, Nvidia announced that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion. The agreement states that about $11.9 billion will be paid to the Hugging Face shareholders, and the remainder, up to $1 billion, will be given out as equity-based retention awards to employees in the form of incentive compensation. The closing of the transaction is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated to occur in the first half of 2027.\\nNvidia has stated that Hugging Face will continue to be open and interoperable with various models, frameworks and clouds. Hugging Face is already one of the most popular destinations for open AI models. For Nvidia, this means a stronger software moat, through CUDA, and an earlier position in the developer workflow.\\nThere are concerns, however, that through this acquisition, Nvidia will work to favor its chips, and deteriorate the relationships Hugging Face previously had with various clouds. Because of this and other concerns, this acquisition and many like it, will be under strong regulatory scrutiny. Although Nvidia will expand its software moat with this acquisition, it has created a similar challenge for itself in terms of regulation and other ecosystem management.\\nQ2 Revenue More Than Doubled\\nNvidia achieved fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, a YoY increase of 106% and an 18% increase from the prior quarter. Data Center revenue was $89 billion, a YoY increase of 117% and 18%. GAAP operating income increased by 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income increased 126% to $59.7 billion.\\nGAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS was $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, compared to 72.4% and 72.5% respectively in the prior year. The combination of triple-digit revenue growth and an increasing gross margin shows Nvidia is converting demand into exceptional profitability at scale, and shows the health of the business.\\nThat is the part of the story I find most impressive. Nvidia is not merely shipping more accelerators. Customers are moving toward Blackwell and Rubin type systems that bundle a variety of compute and networking components as well as software.\\nVera Rubin Is Ramping Into Full Production\\nBlackwell remains the primary source of revenue, but Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production. The company has racks deployed with partners like CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius. Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform are also arriving at large AI factories, Nvidia says.\\nThe annual product cadence is Nvidia’s biggest competitive edge. Customers deploying Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra are already preparing infrastructure for Rubin, which further decreases the risk of a long transition gap between product generations. It also pushes the technical bar higher for AMD and for custom accelerators built by hyperscalers.\\nNvidia is broadening its product portfolio beyond GPUs. The company unveiled a standalone AI CPU, called Vera, to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads. Like management expects, if Vera gains traction in the market, it could further expand Nvidia’s customer base beyond its existing GPU customer base.\\nThe $108 Billion Q3 Guide Sets Another High Bar\\nNvidia expects about $108B in revenue for fiscal Q3 2027 (plus or minus 2%). That represents a big jump from the $96.2B in revenue generated in Q2 2027. Management expects non-GAAP gross margins to be approximately 74% and GAAP gross margins to be about the same (plus or minus 50 basis points).\\nMost importantly, Nvidia's guidance assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China. Without the Chinese Data Center market, the guidance still seems very optimistic. China was one of Nvidia’s largest markets for accelerators prior to the export ban. Export restrictions mean Nvidia has very little access to sell its high-end accelerators to the market. So far, the constraint has not been a major issue because of the strong demand from U.S. hyperscalers, as well as, sovereign AI projects, startups, and enterprises.\\nThe Q3 guidance is now the most important near-term benchmark. An actual result above $108 billion with margins close to 74% would show Blackwell Ultra demand is very strong and Rubin's production ramp remains on track. A significant miss would be a warning sign because expectations are higher and the stock is trading at all-time highs.\\nAI Financing Is Becoming a New Systemic Risk\\nNvidia is partnering with major capital providers to establish independent financing platforms designed to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time. The partners include Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR. Nvidia states the purpose of these financing platforms is to provide funding for ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia.\\nAccording to Reuters, lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment due to the fact that advanced GPUs can quickly become obsolete as a new generation is released. Banks typically model the useful economic life of equipment at 3 to 4 years, while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer. If equipment is expected to be productive for a shorter period of time, lenders are unwilling to provide as much funding against the equipment, and may require other collateral.\\nThis is becoming a major concern because the AI boom is also becoming a capital boom. If developers and infrastructure companies are too reliant on debt to finance Nvidia systems, collections can slow even if the demand for compute remains.\\nPower May Become a Bigger Constraint Than Chip Demand\\nAccording to Morgan Stanley this week, Nvidia and Broadcom seem relatively protected from the current U.S. data center power shortage, but other constraints may affect broader deployment. Nvidia can still get orders even if customers don't have the means to build out the power infrastructure to support the order.\\nThe power issue is structural. Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta and Oracle, and other hyperscale/cloud companies are investing heavily to secure long term nuclear, natural gas and renewable energy sources. That's because the companies will need to build out new data centers and supporting infrastructure to meet customer demand. Nvidia's constrains are tied to how quickly the rest of the data center infrastructure ecosystem can be expanded.\\nIn my opinion, this is one of the most clear risks to the overall market and offers a fair valuation on Nvidia shares given the other constraining factors.\\nValuation Still Requires Near-Perfect Execution\\nAt the recent close, Nvidia’s valuation was approximately $5.77 trillion. Stock Analysis shows a trailing P/E ratio of close to 30 and forward P/E of 19. Nvidia’s valuation is showing extreme expectations for future growth and not reflective of the semiconductor industry.\\nInvestors have re-rated Nvidia as the core infrastructure and global platform for artificial intelligence (AI). Similar valuations to Nvidia’s current growth rates are not that extreme, but there is little room for slowdowns. Currently, Nvidia is experiencing exceptionally strong demand for its products. Nvidia must not only continue the strong demand for its products, but also manage the product transition from Blackwell to Rubin. Finally, they must maintain gross margins, and ensure customers can afford and have the infrastructure to support the systems they are ordering. Any major disruption to these areas could affect the positive sentiment around Nvidia.\\nNvidia Technical Analysis: NVDA Breaks $233.17 as Bulls Target $242.15\\nNvidia closed at $238.90 on October 5 after breaking the $233.17 resistance level and hitting an all-time high of $240.10 on the day. Nvidia is clearly above both of its moving averages and printing higher lows and higher highs. A bullish trend line from the July lows also supports the structure.\\nNvidia Stock Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\\nNVDA’s RSI is in overbought territory at 72 after touching 62 on its signal line. The overbought conditions and the approach toward the $242.15 resistance level increase the chances of minor consolidation before resuming the upside move.\\nThe upside momentum would require a 4-hour close above the $242.15 resistance level, with the next target at $248.82. Beyond that, the next target is $255.85. On the downside, a break below the $233.17 would be a bearish sign and the 50 period moving average at $225.01 would also provide support. The next major support after that would be the cluster between $222.01 and $220.21.\\nMy bullish outlook is based on Nvidia holding above $233.17. With the overbought conditions and RSI, I would expect a retrace before continuing higher.\\n- Latest completed close: $238.90\\n- Major support levels: $233.17, $225.01, then $222.01 to $220.21\\n- Major resistance levels: $242.15, $248.82, then $255.85\\n- RSI: approximately 72, overbought but bullish\\n- Breakout target: $248.82 above a confirmed $242.15 break\\nWhy is Nvidia stock in focus now?\\nNvidia’s current quarter is ablaze because fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubled, Data Center growth was above 100%, Vera Rubin is in full production, and management is guiding for another record quarter with $108 billion of revenue. Also, Nvidia is expanding its software ecosystem through the proposed Hugging Face acquisition while creating new ways to finance AI infrastructure.\\nWhat level confirms a stronger NVDA breakout?\\nA 4-hour close above $242.15 confirms a bullish extension, and a target of $248.82 becomes probable. A breakdown of $233.17 cancels the current NVDA breakout and increases the probability of a deeper retest to $225.01.\\nNvidia is entering October with perhaps the strongest Earnings potential in global technology. Q2 revenue was up 106%, the Data Center was up 117%, the gross margin was 75%, and management expects another record in Q3. Vera Rubin going into production, while Blackwell Ultra continues to scale, gives Nvidia the potential to keep its product lead.\\nThe risks now are more financing related. The availability of power for Data Centers, China restrictions, customer deployments, and the pace of AI rollouts will all play a big role in determining just how quickly Nvidia can meet customer demand. NVDA is still bullish above $233.17, however the overbought condition of the RSI on the 4-hour chart suggests a move to the downside and higher volatility in the short term is probable. The long term outlook of AI remains strong.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin (next-generation rack-scale AI) platform reach volume customer shipments and recognized revenue during fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Volume shipments and a first material revenue contribution from Vera Rubin-based systems, ramping alongside Blackwell Ultra, by the fiscal 2027 year-end.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); the item reports Rubin already ramping into full production with racks deployed at named cloud partners as of October 2026 and ties the fiscal Q3 2027 revenue guide to the ramp.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia complete its approximately $12.93 billion acquisition of Hugging Face by the anticipated first-half 2027 closing?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Closing of the announced Hugging Face acquisition after customary conditions and regulatory approvals.\",\n      \"period\": \"First half of 2027, the closing window the item anticipates (calendar year).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will US export controls allow Nvidia to resume material data center accelerator sales in China during fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Any licence grant or easing of export restrictions that lets Nvidia record Data Center compute revenue from China, versus the zero-China assumption embedded in its guidance.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for this pending policy question; the item reports the export restriction in force.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, with Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially. GAAP operating income rose 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income rose 126% to $59.7 billion; GAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, versus 72.4% GAAP and 72.5% non-GAAP in the prior year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a step up from the $96.2 billion recorded in fiscal Q2 2027, with non-GAAP gross margin of approximately 74% and GAAP gross margin about the same, plus or minus 50 basis points. The guidance assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\",\n       \"opened here: A-T3\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia disclosed on September 28 that its board increased the share repurchase authorization by an additional $150 billion, which the article calls the largest such increase in history; management expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028 and about $235 billion remains available under it.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion granted as equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia said Blackwell remains its primary revenue source while Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with the company's racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia unveiled a standalone AI CPU called Vera, intended to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads; the item presents it as a broadening of Nvidia's product portfolio beyond GPUs, without shipment dates or volumes.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Morgan Stanley said this week that Nvidia and Broadcom look relatively protected from the current U.S. data center power shortage, though other constraints may affect broader deployment, and that Nvidia can still take orders even if customers lack the means to build the power infrastructure to support them.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia said it is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms designed to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia stating the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than to shift the entire cost onto Nvidia.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S9\",\n      \"statement\": \"According to Reuters, lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can quickly become obsolete as a new generation is released; banks typically model a 3 to 4 year useful economic life for the equipment while Nvidia says the life can be much longer, and shorter assumed lives lead lenders to lend less against the equipment and possibly require other collateral.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S10\",\n      \"statement\": \"The item reports that China was one of Nvidia's largest accelerator markets before the export ban, that export restrictions leave Nvidia very little access to sell its high-end accelerators there, and that the constraint has so far not been a major issue because of demand from U.S. hyperscalers, sovereign AI projects, startups and enterprises.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T3\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially. GAAP operating income rose 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income rose 126% to $59.7 billion; GAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS was $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, versus 72.4% GAAP and 72.5% non-GAAP in the prior year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and non-GAAP gross margin to approximately 74%, with GAAP gross margin about the same plus or minus 50 basis points; the guidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-3\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion on September 28, the largest such increase in history; management expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028, with about $235 billion remaining under the authorization.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion in equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-6\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms intended to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia saying the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Reuters reported that lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can become obsolete quickly; banks typically model a 3-to-4-year useful economic life while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer, which can reduce funding against the equipment and lead lenders to require other collateral.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia unveiled a standalone AI CPU called Vera to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads, saying it could further expand Nvidia's customer base beyond its existing GPU customers.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 5,\n  \"title\": \"ASML vs. Nvidia: Which AI Semiconductor Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-06\",\n  \"text\": \"As the artificial intelligence (AI) boom matures, investors can choose between companies that design AI chips and those that supply the specialized machinery required to manufacture them. Is ASML Holding ( ASML +1.86% ) or Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) the better play?\\nASML is the world's only manufacturer of extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography systems, the machines used to produce leading-edge chips. Nvidia operates at the other end of the supply chain, designing graphics processing units (GPUs) that train and run AI models in data centers. Both offer a way to invest in AI stocks , though they are growing at very different rates.\\nASML designs and manufactures the lithography systems that chipmakers use to print circuit patterns onto silicon wafers. It stands apart among semiconductor stocks because its EUV systems print the highly complex layers of advanced chips found in newer smartphones and AI hardware. The company sells to makers of both logic and memory chips, and its customer base is concentrated. Four customers accounted for about 61% of total net sales in 2025, according to its annual report.\\nASML reports its results in euros, and the figures here are converted to U.S. dollars. In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached about $37.1 billion, up roughly 15.6% from the prior fiscal year. Net income totaled about $10.9 billion for the period, with a net margin of about 29.4%.\\nOn its December 2025 balance sheet, prepared under U.S. GAAP, the debt-to-equity ratio was roughly 0.2x. That means total debt, including both short-term and long-term borrowings, was about a fifth of shareholder equity.\\nThe current ratio, which measures the ability to pay short-term obligations with short-term assets, was roughly 1.3x, and free cash flow reached close to $12.1 billion.\\nNvidia designs GPUs and the software that runs on them, which together are used to train and deploy AI models. In its annual report for fiscal 2026, which ended on Jan. 25, 2026, the company noted that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds risk, as losing one major buyer could hurt financial results.\\nIn fiscal 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. Net income followed suit, climbing to about $120.1 billion during the same period. That resulted in a net margin of about 55.6%, although net income included roughly $8.9 billion in net gains on equity securities.\\nOn its January 2026 balance sheet, the debt-to-equity ratio was about 0.05x on a total-debt basis, indicating limited reliance on borrowed capital relative to equity. Nvidia has borrowed more since then, and its July 26, 2026, balance sheet shows about $33.4 billion of debt, or roughly 0.15x equity.\\nThe current ratio at the end of fiscal 2026 was approximately 3.9x, indicating ample liquidity to cover near-term obligations. Free cash flow for fiscal 2026 was approximately $96.7 billion, representing the cash generated from operations minus capital expenditures.\\nASML depends on a narrow supply chain. It relies on external vendors for components in its systems, and each component is obtained from a single supplier or a limited number of suppliers, according to its annual report.\\nExport controls also limit which systems the company can ship to customers in China, which accounted for about 29% of total net sales in 2025. In addition, ASML is bringing its next-generation High NA EUV systems to market, and research and development costs ran about 14% of total net sales in 2025.\\nNvidia does not manufacture its own chips. It relies on foundries such as Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing ( TSM +2.34% ) to produce its wafers, and its annual report cites long manufacturing lead times and dependence on third-party suppliers as risks. Export controls have also been costly.\\nA U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and Nvidia said it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market as of the end of that fiscal year.\\nAdditionally, the company competes with large cloud companies such as Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) and Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) that design their own AI hardware.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple even though its price-to-sales (P/S) ratio is higher.\\nThe forward P/E compares the stock price to future earnings estimates, while the P/S ratio measures the stock price against sales over the past 12 months.\\nNvidia looks like the better buy to me in 2026, with a caveat that applies to both stocks. Each depends on the same wave of AI data center spending, so I'd own either only as part of a diversified portfolio held for five years or more.\\nNvidia is growing faster and trades at a lower multiple of expected earnings. Revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026.\\nManagement then guided for about $108 billion in the fiscal third quarter without assuming any data center compute revenue from China. On Sept. 28, the company added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization, which says a good deal about the cash this business generates.\\nThe risk is concentration. Two customers accounted for more than a third of fiscal 2026 revenue, and large cloud companies are designing their own AI chips.\\nASML deserves more than a passing mention. In July, management raised its 2026 sales outlook to a range roughly a third above its 2025 total, and it plans to add EUV capacity for 2027. Its machines are used to make leading-edge chips, regardless of who designs them, giving ASML a stake in AI's growth even if Nvidia's rivals gain ground.\\nThe shares have gained more than 80% over the past year, though, and the higher earnings multiple appears to reflect much of that strength. For investors who already own Nvidia, ASML still looks like a worthy long-term holding.\\nASML supplies the machines that advanced chipmaking depends on, while Nvidia generates nearly six times the revenue at close to double the net margin.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Oct 6, 2026 at 8:32AM EST\\nNvidia designs the GPUs used to train and run AI models, and its revenue grew roughly 65% in fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026.\\nTwo customers accounted for 36% of Nvidia's fiscal 2026 revenue, and four customers made up about 61% of ASML's 2025 sales.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's annual report for fiscal 2026 disclosed that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively; the article also reports that the two customers combined for 36% of Nvidia's fiscal 2026 revenue.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, and net income of about $120.1 billion, a net margin of about 55.6%; net income included roughly $8.9 billion of net gains on equity securities.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia management guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to about $108 billion, without assuming any data center compute revenue from China.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"On Sept. 28, 2026, Nvidia added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"A U.S. license requirement on Nvidia's H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and Nvidia said it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market as of the end of that fiscal year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 annual report figures, as cited by Fool.com: revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year; net income of about $120.1 billion; a net margin of about 55.6%; and net income that included roughly $8.9 billion of net gains on equity securities.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's balance sheet shifted toward more borrowing: the debt-to-equity ratio was about 0.05x on a total-debt basis at January 2026, and Fool.com reports NVIDIA has borrowed more since, with its July 26, 2026 balance sheet showing about $33.4 billion of debt, or roughly 0.15x equity.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, according to Fool.com's recap of the reported results.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management guided for about $108 billion of revenue in the fiscal third quarter, and the guidance does not assume any data center compute revenue from China.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-3\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"On Sept. 28, 2026, NVIDIA added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization, a board decision Fool.com links to the company's cash generation.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said a U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and that as of the end of that fiscal year it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-3\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 annual report disclosed that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, i.e. about 36% combined.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"ASML management raised its 2026 sales outlook in July to a range roughly a third above its 2025 total and plans to add extreme-ultraviolet (EUV) lithography capacity for 2027, an upstream capacity signal for leading-edge chip output.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 6,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia (NVDA): Is There Still Room to Run?\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-07\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) went nowhere for most of a year, then broke out in August and now trades at a record high. Ken Fisher's Fisher Asset Management is Nvidia's largest hedge fund holder as of the second quarter, with a stake worth about $18.2 billion, or 5.42% of its portfolio, after raising it by 3% in the second quarter. Nvidia ranks second in our list of his 10 best AI stocks. Click here to see the other nine picks .\\nBulls say demand is still growing faster than Nvidia can build chips. AI is moving from training models to running them for businesses and consumers, and every new agent and app adds to the computing power needed. Weighing the AI chip giants? Read our comparison of Nvidia and Broadcom to see which is the better buy now . Management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply holds that number back. Its CEO has said growth would come close to 100% if Nvidia could make every chip customers want.\\nNvidia is also tackling the worry that matters most to the market, which is money. It lined up large lenders, including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo, to finance customers' data centers, and it offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value. Its CUDA software lets the same chips handle both training and inference, which helps them keep their value, and its revenue per gigawatt of data center capacity has risen with each chip generation, according to its CEO. Even Stanley Druckenmiller has said he regrets selling Nvidia too early. Read what he said in our list of his 10 best AI stocks . Management also recently announced a record increase to its share buyback.\\nMargins are the first worry. Nvidia cut its gross margin outlook for fiscal 2028 to 72% to 73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected. Its newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter, and industry reports say the ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. A delay would push sales out. Want more AI ideas? See our list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy before they explode .\\nThe bigger risk sits with its customers. Big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter, their debt is rising, and Morgan Stanley estimates the cash they generate will cover only part of the data center spending planned through 2028. Nvidia is helping fill that gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand now depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. A default by a large customer such as OpenAI would test those guarantees.\\nCompetition is also growing. AMD is pushing harder, and cloud companies are building their own chips with partners like Broadcom. A startup backed by former Intel CEO Pat Gelsinger is also going after Nvidia's AI networking business. See how it plans to challenge Nvidia's edge . That is the risk for investors in Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA).\\nNvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) trades at a forward P/E of 25.7, only slightly above the sector median of 23.8 and about 39% below its own five-year average of 42.2. Analysts expect earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028, which puts the P/E at about 15 on fiscal 2028 earnings. The PEG ratio, which compares the P/E to growth, is 0.49 against 1.29 for the sector, so Nvidia costs less than half as much per point of growth as a typical stock in its sector. Analysts raised their fiscal 2028 estimate by about 24% over the past 90 days, and the stock looks undervalued for its growth, as long as Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) delivers on those forecasts.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply constraints hold that number back; its CEO said growth would come close to 100% if NVIDIA could make every chip customers want.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA lined up large lenders including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo to finance customers' data centers and offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value; the article says NVIDIA is helping fill a customer funding gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The article reports that big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter and their debt is rising; Morgan Stanley estimates their cash generation will cover only part of the data-center spending planned through 2028.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA cut its gross margin outlook for fiscal 2028 to 72%–73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"The article expects NVIDIA's newest chip, Rubin, to make up only about 20% of data-center revenue in the current quarter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Industry reports, as cited by the article, say Rubin's ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Analysts expect NVIDIA's earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028; the article also says analysts raised their fiscal 2028 estimate by about 24% over the past 90 days.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management recently announced a record increase to the company's share buyback, according to the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"How much credit support will Nvidia have extended to its customers' data-center financing — residual-value guarantees and similar backstops — as disclosed in its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The dollar amount of Nvidia's guarantees/financing commitments to customers (including loss coverage of up to 25% if chips lose value) disclosed in its next Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; 10-K expected around February 2027) — the item describes the guarantee/financing support as already in place, so an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's newest data-center chip, Rubin, complete its volume ramp on the planned 2026 schedule, or slip into 2027 because of memory shortages?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Rubin's ramp stays in calendar 2026 or slips into 2027, as indicated by Rubin's share of Nvidia's data-center revenue and management commentary on supply.\",\n      \"period\": \"Through calendar 2027: whether the Rubin ramp holds its 2026 schedule or slips into 2027 (the slip window the item reports).\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply constraints hold that figure back; its CEO has said growth would come close to 100% if Nvidia could make every chip customers want.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia cut its fiscal 2028 gross margin outlook to 72% to 73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue in the current quarter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Industry reports say the ramp of Nvidia's Rubin chip could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages, and a delay would push sales out.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia lined up large lenders including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo to finance customers' data centers, and it offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value; the article says Nvidia is filling part of customers' funding gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand now depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter and their debt is rising, and Morgan Stanley estimates the cash they generate will cover only part of the data center spending they have planned through 2028.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Analysts expect Nvidia's earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Analysts raised their fiscal 2028 earnings estimate for Nvidia by about 24% over the 90 days before the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S9\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's management recently announced a record increase to its share buyback, according to the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 7,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia (NVDA)’s $20 Billion Groq Deal Faces a Legal Test. Does It Matter for NVDA’s Valuation?\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-08\",\n  \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s $20 billion deal for Groq's AI technology is facing a new legal challenge just as that technology begins contributing to Nvidia's push into inference.\\nA lawsuit filed by two former Groq engineers in Delaware alleges that Groq's board improperly structured the 2025 transaction, which consisted of a $17 billion licensing agreement and a separate $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool for certain engineers who joined the chipmaker. The plaintiffs argue that the arrangement effectively transferred Groq's core technology and much of its engineering talent without treating the transaction as a conventional acquisition. Groq has called the lawsuit meritless.\\nFor Nvidia investors, however, the bigger question is not simply whether the transaction faces litigation. It is whether Groq has become important enough to Nvidia's next phase of growth for that legal and regulatory uncertainty to matter. NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) also made our latest list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy in October , but another AI stock ranked even higher.\\nGroq Is Already Moving From Licensing Deal to Product\\nThat distinction matters because Groq is no longer a speculative technology bet.\\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later this quarter. Nvidia said the system achieved 3,400 output tokens per second in an Artificial Analysis benchmark, delivering four times faster responsiveness than the nearest alternative platform.\\nGroq technology is also part of Nvidia's broader Vera Rubin platform. Management estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt has expanded from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans GPUs, CPUs, networking, and Groq LPUs.\\nHowever, Jensen Huang has indicated that Groq accelerators primarily target specialized, high-speed inference workloads, while Vera Rubin NVL72 remains the company's broader AI training and inference platform.\\nThat makes the lawsuit more relevant than the $20 billion headline alone indicates. Nvidia is trying to defend its dominance as AI spending increasingly moves from training toward inference, where specialized architectures and custom silicon represent a more meaningful competitive threat.\\nThat competitive shift also puts Nvidia's position against Broadcom under greater scrutiny, particularly as investors weigh which AI chip stock is better positioned as spending expands beyond traditional GPUs .\\nPiper Sandler captured that opportunity in September when it initiated NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) at Overweight with a $300 price target, describing the company as the \\\"enterprise inference king.\\\"\\nNvidia generated $96 billion of revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, more than double the prior-year level, while Data Center revenue reached $89 billion. Management expects approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says that outlook is supply-constrained.\\nMorgan Stanley sees potential upside even to that forecast. The firm argued on October 2 that underlying demand could support growth closer to 100%, while Nvidia's broad customer base and ability to generate more tokens per gigawatt leave it well positioned as power and data-center capacity become the next AI bottlenecks. Morgan Stanley has an Overweight rating and $300 target.\\nThose expectations explain why NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s valuation looks very different depending on how far forward investors look.\\nThe same valuation question becomes even more pronounced when Nvidia is compared with another megacap technology leader, raising the question of whether NVDA or Apple offers the stronger valuation case at current expectations .\\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) traded at a 24.88 times forward P/E as of October 6. Consensus estimates call for EPS growth of 95.23% in fiscal 2027 and another 69.66% in fiscal 2028, bringing estimated P/E down from 25.65 times fiscal 2027 earnings to just 15.12 times fiscal 2028 earnings.\\nPut simply, Nvidia does not necessarily look expensive if those earnings materialize. The risk is that its valuation increasingly depends on maintaining extraordinary growth as inference becomes a larger part of the AI market.\\nFor now, the Groq litigation does not appear large enough on its own to challenge Nvidia's earnings trajectory. But the dispute raises a broader issue.\\nThe plaintiffs acknowledge that Delaware courts have not directly determined whether acqui-hire transactions like Groq's should face traditional M&A scrutiny. Separately, the U.S. Department of Justice was reportedly probing Nvidia's Groq transaction, while FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has said the agency would examine deal structures potentially designed to escape conventional merger review.\\nThat matters because Nvidia did not merely obtain Groq technology. It also hired much of the engineering team behind it without buying the entire company. Greater scrutiny of that structure could affect how Nvidia and other technology companies acquire strategically important AI capabilities in the future.\\nBefore the lawsuit emerged, hedge fund interest in Nvidia had increased. The number of hedge funds bullish on NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) increased from 275 to 285 in Q2, with Fisher Asset Management increasing its position 3%, AQR Capital Management 18%, and Arrowstreet Capital 10%.\\nThe near-term test, therefore, is not the lawsuit itself. It is whether Groq 3 LPX scales commercially and helps Nvidia extend its dominance from AI training into inference. If it does, the $20 billion transaction becomes easier to justify against the earnings growth Nvidia is expected to deliver. If legal or regulatory scrutiny begins interfering with that commercialization, the Groq dispute becomes much more relevant to NVDA's valuation.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's Groq 3 LPX — its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology — reach volume customer shipments, and therefore first revenue recognition, by the time NVIDIA reports fiscal Q3 2027 results?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Groq 3 LPX moves from 'full production' to volume shipments in the quarter ending late October 2026, versus slipping, and whether the LPU line begins contributing revenue as management expects.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026), checked at NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 results in November 2026 — the item states volume shipments are expected 'later this quarter' in an 8 October 2026 article, which fixes the check date.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Two former Groq engineers filed a lawsuit in Delaware alleging that Groq's board improperly structured the 2025 transaction through which NVIDIA obtained Groq's AI technology — a $17 billion licensing agreement plus a separate $3 billion NVIDIA stock bonus pool for engineers who joined the chipmaker — arguing the arrangement transferred Groq's core technology and much of its engineering talent without treating the deal as a conventional acquisition; Groq called the lawsuit meritless. NVDA's $20 billion deal for Groq's technology is described as facing a new legal challenge.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The U.S. Department of Justice was reportedly probing NVIDIA's Groq transaction, and FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has said the agency would examine deal structures potentially designed to escape conventional merger review, according to the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later in the quarter (NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027).\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management estimates its revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI compute has expanded from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans GPUs, CPUs, networking and Groq LPUs.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA generated $96 billion of revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, more than double the prior-year level, with Data Center revenue reaching $89 billion.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management expects approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says that outlook is supply-constrained.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Morgan Stanley argued on October 2 that underlying demand could support NVIDIA revenue growth closer to 100%, above management's roughly 70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast, citing Nvidia's broad customer base and its ability to generate more tokens per gigawatt.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Consensus estimates call for NVIDIA EPS growth of 95.23% in fiscal 2027 and another 69.66% in fiscal 2028, which would bring the estimated P/E down from 25.65 times fiscal 2027 earnings to 15.12 times fiscal 2028 earnings.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Groq 3 LPX rack-scale LPU system reach volume shipments by the end of its fiscal Q3 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"First volume shipments of Groq 3 LPX, Nvidia's first rack-scale LPU system built on Groq's high-interactivity technology, confirmed by Nvidia.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026) — the item's 'later this quarter' for volume shipments, with the item dated 8 October 2026.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"Will the U.S. Department of Justice or FTC bring a formal enforcement action, or require changes to Nvidia's 2025 Groq licensing/acqui-hire transaction, by Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"A formal DOJ/FTC challenge or settlement requiring restructuring of the $20 billion Groq transaction ($17 billion licensing agreement plus $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool).\",\n      \"period\": \"Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027 (late January 2027) — set by rule as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory matter, since the item gives no date for the probe or any decision.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Two former Groq engineers filed a lawsuit in Delaware alleging that Groq's board improperly structured its 2025 transaction with Nvidia — a $20 billion deal consisting of a $17 billion licensing agreement and a separate $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool for certain engineers who joined the chipmaker — effectively transferring Groq's core technology and much of its engineering talent without treating the transaction as a conventional acquisition.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The U.S. Department of Justice was reportedly probing Nvidia's Groq transaction, according to the article; no scope, timing or named source for the probe is given.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson said the agency would examine deal structures potentially designed to escape conventional merger review, a statement bearing on the acqui-hire structure Nvidia used for Groq.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later this quarter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Jensen Huang has indicated that Groq accelerators primarily target specialised, high-speed inference workloads, while Vera Rubin NVL72 remains Nvidia's broader AI training and inference platform.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia generated $96 billion of revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, more than double the prior-year level, with Data Center revenue reaching $89 billion.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia management expects approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says that outlook is supply-constrained.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia management estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt has expanded from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans GPUs, CPUs, networking and Groq LPUs.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S9\",\n      \"statement\": \"Morgan Stanley argued on October 2 that underlying demand could support Nvidia revenue growth closer to 100%, upside to management's approximately 70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast, citing Nvidia's broad customer base and its tokens per gigawatt as power and data-center capacity become the bottlenecks.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S10\",\n      \"statement\": \"Consensus estimates call for Nvidia EPS growth of 95.23% in fiscal 2027 and another 69.66% in fiscal 2028.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 8,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia GPUs are everywhere. Here are the ways companies are accessing them\",\n  \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-10\",\n  \"text\": \"The demand has led to the blossoming of an industry that hyperscalers once had to themselves, including lucrative one-off arrangements and asset-light strategies.\\nOver 300 neoclouds now rent GPUs to businesses, according to a review from researcher SemiAnalysis, up 55% in under 11 months.\\nMarc Benioff, chief executive officer of Salesforce Inc., left, and Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., during the 2026 Dreamforce conference in San Francisco, California, US, on Tuesday, Sept. 15, 2026.\\nNvidia GPUs are the most sought-after processors in AI, and they're in such demand that the chipmaker's stock climbed to yet another record this week, lifting its market cap close to $6 trillion.\\nCustomers can now shop around for access to the chips at the giant clouds from Amazon, Microsoft and Google, as well as at so-called neoclouds like CoreWeave. They can also go to various online marketplaces or even buy the costly hardware directly.\\nFor Nvidia, it all adds up to unrelenting growth, as management anticipates $108 billion in revenue for the October quarter, which would mark an 89% year-over-year jump.\\nBut the paradox of choice can be a headache for companies needing computing power yesterday.\\nWhile cloud infrastructure providers have ranked at the top of Nvidia's customer list for several years, the business is diversifying. Five clients accounted for at least 10% of Nvidia's accounts receivable in July quarter, up from three in January, according to a filing .\\nIndustry research firm SemiAnalysis counted 323 Nvidia GPU providers as of September, up from 209 less than 11 months earlier.\\n\\\"You're going to see a whole new crop of really, really exciting neoclouds with hundreds of billions of dollars backlog together,\\\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at a Goldman Sachs tech conference in San Francisco last month.\\nHere's a rundown of the various options for accessing GPUs, and why each might make sense:\\nMany big companies spend tens of millions of dollars per year on a smorgasbord of cloud services from Amazon, Google and Microsoft. Since the 2022 launch of ChatGPT, companies have increasingly turned to the hyperscalers for GPUs so they can run generative AI workloads.\\nThe top cloud providers come with a reputation advantage. If a software company relies on Amazon and Microsoft for GPUs and other capabilities, it won't need to panic about prospective customers questioning its suppliers.\\nMicrosoft Azure signage on the exhibition floor during the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, on March 18, 2026.\\n\\\"When you're talking to enterprises, your subprocessor had better be Azure,\\\" said Bindu Reddy, CEO of AI assistant startup Abacus, referring to Microsoft's cloud infrastructure.\\nIn the past year, leading AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed to spending over $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, which controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025, according to industry researcher Gartner.\\n\\\"Hyperscalers are in a good position to show trust to the enterprises because of their 10-plus years of full-stack capabilities,\\\" said Gartner analyst Hardeep Singh. But hyperscalers don't always have as many GPUs as enterprises require, he said.\\nAndy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, told analysts in July that the retailer and cloud pioneer won't be able to serve all the demand it foresees this year.\\n\\\"I believe this dynamic will also be true in 2027,\\\" he said.\\nIf the hyperscalers were adequate, neoclouds wouldn't be proliferating.\\nModal, a startup operating virtual sandboxes where AI agents work independent of main IT environments, went from running on the hyperscalers to signing up with the major neoclouds, and now it uses 25 of them, said CEO Erik Bernhardsson.\\n\\\"You can get a few hundred GPUs or maybe a thousand, but at our scale, we needed way more GPUs,\\\" he said.\\nThe hyperscalers themselves are also chasing the neoclouds. Google and Microsoft have started tapping CoreWeave, even as they all compete with one another.\\n\\\"Some of the hyperscalers have approached us about taking care of customers they're worried about because they don't have the ability to service those customers when they need it,\\\" said Marc Boroditsky, chief revenue officer of Nebius, a Netherlands-based neocloud with operations in the U.S.\\nVideo generation startup Reactor uses GPUs through Nebius and hyperscalers, said CEO Alberto Taiuti. The location of data centers matters, he said, because Reactor wants user-created videos to show up right away. Nebius delivers the specific GPUs Reactor needs, solid customer service and sufficient hardware and software, at a good price, Taiuti said.\\nMichael Intrator, co-founder and CEO of CoreWeave, speaks during an interview with CNBC on the floor at the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in New York City, U.S., Feb. 27, 2026.\\nThe most visible neoclouds can require some upfront payment, and chips might not come online for months, because providers raise funding based on contracts and set up the data center equipment, Bernhardsson said.\\nIt would be difficult for CoreWeave to turn over 10,000 GPUs to a new customer with one day's notice, said Chen Goldberg, an executive vice president. CoreWeave's near-term capacity remains essentially sold out, CEO Mike Intrator said on the company's August earnings call.\\nIf companies want GPUs immediately, they might have to go beyond the name brands. Some neoclouds aren't household names because they target specific countries, which can be viable in certain cases.\\n\\\"Capacity right now is tight, and your relationships with your suppliers is actually one of the most closely guarded secrets for companies like ours,\\\" said Zhen Lu, CEO of Runpod.\\nSpecialist neoclouds can offer more flexibility than the larger GPU clouds, which often require upfront payments and long-term commitments. Some sell so-called bare-metal GPUs, which give customers more control but also require them to manage more of the technical work themselves.\\nCompanies using these smaller neoclouds share the same concerns: When can they get the GPUs, and at what price? Sunny Smith, co-founder and technology chief at Massed Compute, said customers are often willing to commit to capacity when they expect prices to rise.\\nOracle , one of the world's largest cloud providers, is letting clients bring in their own GPUs. The software maker has more debt than Amazon or Microsoft, and its credit rating is lower so it has less flexibility to go on a GPU spending spree. But it's happy to operate the technology.\\n\\\"As we're generally able to preserve and improve margins in the case of things like bring-your-own-hardware, the ROIC for those types of structures will be even higher,\\\" Oracle CFO Hilary Maxson told analysts on a June earnings call, using the acronym for return on invested capital.\\nOracle hasn't disclosed the names of companies that choose this path. John DiFucci, a Guggenheim Securities analyst who recommends buying Oracle shares, said it would make sense for Advanced Micro Devices and Nvidia, the top two producers of GPUs, to bring their own to Oracle.\\nInstead of buying thousands of GPUs, early-stage startups with limited capital can borrow them for hours at a time for less through clouds. And for companies with heavy-duty computing needs, Oracle's new route might be more attractive than building whole data centers. OpenAI committed to spending over $300 billion with Oracle over five years, but it hasn't mentioned anything about bringing in GPUs.\\nOpenAI declined to comment.\\nThe method might make sense for companies that have the capital to purchase AI chips but don't have enough power, data center space or skilled labor. Like its hyperscaler peers, Oracle works hard to secure all three in healthy quantities.\\nAnother emerging option is to cut large deals with firms that have truckloads of GPUs for rent.\\nSpaceX arranged to turn over excess capacity in separate deals with hyperscaler Google and open-source startup Reflection .\\nIn April, SpaceX agreed to provide Cursor with GPUs and then bought the AI coding startup outright for $60 billion. And in May, SpaceX landed a deal to rent GPUs to Anthropic for $1.25 billion each month through mid-2029. That's more than what most startups can afford.\\nFor SpaceX, though, the numbers work out nicely.\\n\\\"The current economics have translated into a less than one-year payback on our new capital deployments for compute,\\\" Bret Johnsen, the company's finance chief, told analysts in August.\\nIt's not only SpaceX. In July CNBC reported that Meta was working to form a cloud unit that could sell AI computing power.\\nMeanwhile, companies continue to install GPU-filled servers in on-premises data centers the old-school way as CEOs work to balance capability with cost control.\\nRevenue nearly doubled in the enterprise and small and medium business parts of hardware maker Lenovo's Infrastructure Solutions Group during the June quarter. \\\"We're seeing more and more enterprises now starting to say, 'How do I bring AI into my four walls?'\\\" said Vlad Rozanovich, a senior vice president.\\nThe hourly spot price for an Nvidia B200 GPU has more than doubled since March, according to data from Ornn, a startup that maintains indexes.\\nCollaboration software maker Dropbox relies on GPUs in its data centers, CEO Ashraf Alkarmi said.\\n\\\"If we want to do a lot more, I think our supply chain connections will still be beneficial and a structural advantage,\\\" he said.\\nEverpure , which sells data center storage hardware and software, has acquired its own GPUs, to run open-weight AI models for the company's software engineers, said CEO Charlie Giancarlo.\\n\\\"In a very dynamic pricing environment, it's always good to have multiple sources that you can go to,\\\" he said.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, compared with the more-than-doubling since March 2026 reported for the Ornn index?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The end-2026 hourly spot rental rate for an Nvidia B200 GPU in the secondary/neocloud rental market, as tracked by Ornn's index, versus its level in March 2026 — a gauge of GPU scarcity, neocloud economics and hence the durability of demand for Nvidia data-center GPUs.\",\n      \"period\": \"End-December 2026 (calendar 2026, the 'this year' of this 10 October 2026 item); settled at the release of Ornn's B200 rental-price index for December 2026.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis counted 323 providers renting out Nvidia GPUs as of September 2026, up from 209 less than 11 months earlier — a 55% increase in what CNBC calls neoclouds. The count measures the number of GPU-rental providers, not Nvidia's revenue or GPU volumes.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia management anticipates $108 billion of revenue in the October quarter (the third quarter of its fiscal year), which would mark an 89% year-over-year jump, CNBC reported. This is company guidance, not a reported result.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia disclosed in a filing that five clients each accounted for at least 10% of its accounts receivable in the July quarter, up from three such clients in January — customer concentration in receivables increased over that period.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at a Goldman Sachs technology conference in San Francisco in September 2026 ('last month' relative to the 10 October 2026 article), predicted a new crop of neoclouds holding 'hundreds of billions of dollars backlog together.' The backlog figure is a management characterisation of contracted demand among GPU-rental providers, not a disclosed Nvidia figure.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed over the past year to spending more than $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, CNBC reported, citing Gartner for the fact that the two cloud providers controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025. The commitment figure is a multi-year contracted amount between those parties, not a Nvidia figure and not a 2026 data-center capex number.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Amazon CEO Andy Jassy told analysts in July 2026 that Amazon will not be able to serve all the cloud demand it foresees that year, and said he believes the same dynamic will hold in 2027 — a claim that hyperscaler capacity will remain short of demand this year and next.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Marc Boroditsky, chief revenue officer of Netherlands-based neocloud Nebius, said some hyperscalers have approached Nebius about serving customers they worry they cannot serve when those customers need capacity — a named provider reporting hyperscalers sourcing third-party GPU capacity.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"CoreWeave CEO Mike Intrator said on the neocloud's August 2026 earnings call that CoreWeave's near-term GPU capacity remains essentially sold out.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S9\",\n      \"statement\": \"The hourly spot rental price for an Nvidia B200 GPU has more than doubled since March 2026, according to index provider Ornn, as reported by CNBC on 10 October 2026. The item gives no absolute price level.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S10\",\n      \"statement\": \"Hardware maker Lenovo's Infrastructure Solutions Group nearly doubled revenue in its enterprise and small and medium business segments in the June 2026 quarter, a server vendor's reported result indicating enterprise demand for AI hardware installed on customers' own premises.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S11\",\n      \"statement\": \"Google and Microsoft have started using neocloud CoreWeave's GPU capacity even while competing with it, CNBC reported — hyperscalers sourcing capacity from a third-party GPU cloud.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, after the item reports it more than doubled between March and October 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The year-end 2026 level of the hourly spot rental price for an Nvidia B200 GPU, as tracked by Ornn's index.\",\n      \"period\": \"End of calendar 2026 (December 2026 reading); set from the item's reference to 2026 spot pricing and the year-end index reading.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's management anticipates $108 billion of revenue for the October quarter, which CNBC says would mark an 89% year-over-year jump.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Five clients each accounted for at least 10% of Nvidia's accounts receivable in the July quarter, up from three in January, according to a company filing cited by CNBC.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Research firm SemiAnalysis counted 323 providers renting out Nvidia GPUs as of September 2026, up from 209 less than 11 months earlier, an increase of 55%, according to CNBC's review of its research.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at a Goldman Sachs tech conference in San Francisco in September 2026 ('last month') that a whole new crop of neoclouds will emerge with 'hundreds of billions of dollars backlog together' — a forward-looking claim, not a reported backlog figure.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Amazon CEO Andy Jassy told analysts in July 2026 that Amazon will not be able to serve all the cloud demand it foresees this year, and said he believes the same will be true in 2027.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Google and Microsoft have started renting capacity from neocloud CoreWeave, even as the companies compete with each other, CNBC reported.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Marc Boroditsky, chief revenue officer of neocloud Nebius, said some hyperscalers have approached Nebius about serving customers they are worried about because they cannot service those customers themselves when needed.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"CoreWeave CEO Mike Intrator said on the company's August 2026 earnings call that CoreWeave's near-term GPU capacity remains essentially sold out.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S9\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oracle is letting cloud clients bring in their own GPUs, and Oracle CFO Hilary Maxson told analysts on a June 2026 earnings call that structures such as bring-your-own-hardware let Oracle preserve and improve margins and will earn even higher returns on invested capital.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S10\",\n      \"statement\": \"Lenovo's Infrastructure Solutions Group nearly doubled revenue in the enterprise and small-and-medium-business parts of its business in the June 2026 quarter, and senior vice president Vlad Rozanovich said more enterprises are asking how to bring AI into their own four walls.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S11\",\n      \"statement\": \"The hourly spot price for an Nvidia B200 GPU has more than doubled since March 2026, according to index data from startup Ornn, as reported by CNBC in October 2026.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 9,\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA in Talks to Acquire Reflection AI, Valued at $25 Billion\",\n  \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-10\",\n  \"text\": \"On October 10, 2026, NVIDIA Corp NVDA is reportedly in discussions to acquire the American startup Reflection AI, which specializes in developing open-weight models. This potential acquisition could deepen NVIDIA's investment in the artificial intelligence sector. While the specific terms of the deal are still unclear, Reflection AI was valued at $25 billion during a funding round in March.\\nGF Value™ verdict: NVDA is currently priced at $229.28, which is 43.6% undervalued compared to its GF Value™ of $406.18.\\nGF Score™: 95/100, indicating strong overall performance in various financial metrics.\\nFinancial Strength: Ranked 9/10, reflecting a robust balance sheet.\\nThe news of NVIDIA's potential acquisition of Reflection AI highlights the company's ongoing commitment to enhancing its capabilities in the rapidly evolving artificial intelligence sector. By acquiring a startup that specializes in open-weight models, NVIDIA aims to solidify its position as a leader in AI infrastructure and applications. This strategic move aligns with the company's broader goal of expanding its influence in the tech landscape, particularly as demand for AI solutions continues to surge.\\nNVIDIA is recognized as the world's leading supplier of accelerated computing and AI infrastructure, with a market capitalization of approximately $5.53 trillion. The company designs graphics processing units (GPUs), data-center-scale systems, and networking solutions built around its CUDA software platform. Its offerings cater to various sectors, including gaming, professional visualization, and autonomous vehicles, making it a key player in the semiconductor industry.\\nAccording to GuruFocus, NVIDIA's GF Value™ is calculated at $406.18, indicating that the stock is currently undervalued by approximately 43.6% at its current price of $229.28. This significant margin of safety suggests that investors may have an opportunity to acquire shares at a discount relative to the company's intrinsic value. The current price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio stands at 28.98, which is considerably lower than its 5-year median P/E of 55.91, further supporting the notion that NVDA may be undervalued. For more details, visit the GF Value™ page .\\nThe GF Score™ is a comprehensive measure that evaluates a company's financial strength, profitability, growth potential, valuation, and momentum. NVIDIA's impressive GF Score™ of 95/100 reflects its strong performance across various metrics, particularly in profitability and growth, where it ranks 10/10. However, its valuation rank is lower at 4/10, indicating some caution regarding its current market price relative to its intrinsic value.\\nNVIDIA's strengths lie in its exceptional profitability and growth metrics, which are crucial for long-term success. However, the lower valuation rank suggests that investors should exercise caution and consider the potential for a value trap, as indicated by the GF Valuation label.\\nFor further insights, visit the NVDA stock page .\\nCurrently, 33 gurus hold shares of NVIDIA, with 12 adding to their positions and 20 trimming their holdings in recent quarters. This mixed activity indicates a cautious sentiment among institutional investors. Additionally, insider activity has shown significant selling, with a total of approximately $968 million in insider sales over the past three months. This trend may warrant further investigation by potential investors.\\nThe potential acquisition of Reflection AI could enhance NVIDIA's competitive edge in the AI sector, making it a compelling investment opportunity. However, with a GF Value™ indicating significant undervaluation alongside a cautionary valuation rank, investors should weigh the risks carefully. For those interested in NVIDIA's prospects, the NVDA stock page provides comprehensive data and analysis.\\nNVIDIA's GF Score™ is 95/100, indicating strong overall financial performance across various metrics.\\nNVIDIA is currently undervalued, with a GF Value™ of $406.18 compared to its market price of $229.28, representing a 43.6% discount.\\nNVIDIA's current P/E ratio is 28.98, significantly lower than its 5-year median P/E of 55.91, suggesting it may be undervalued relative to its historical performance.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a definitive agreement to acquire Reflection AI, the open-weight AI model developer reported to be valued at $25 billion in its March 2026 funding round?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"A definitive agreement (or the collapse of talks) for NVIDIA to acquire Reflection AI\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 2028-04-10, set as 18 months from the item date of 2026-10-10 because the acquisition is not yet signed\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA Corp was reportedly in discussions on October 10, 2026 to acquire the American startup Reflection AI, which specializes in developing open-weight AI models; the specific terms of the potential deal were still unclear, and Reflection AI had been valued at $25 billion in a funding round in March.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a definitive agreement and complete an acquisition of Reflection AI — the open-weight AI model startup it is reportedly in talks to buy, valued at $25 billion in a March 2026 funding round — by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the reported talks turn into a signed and closed transaction for Reflection AI (versus talks ending without a deal, or a deal repriced/delayed past the check date), as disclosed in NVIDIA's press releases, Form 8-K and quarterly filings.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 2028-04-10 (18 months after the 10 October 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet signed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"GuruFocus reported on 10 October 2026 that NVIDIA is reportedly in discussions to acquire Reflection AI, an American startup that develops open-weight AI models; the specific deal terms were still unclear, and Reflection AI had been valued at $25 billion in a funding round in March (2026).\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 10,\n  \"title\": \"If I Had a Choice to Invest $10,000 in SpaceX or Nvidia, Here's What I Would Do\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-11\",\n  \"text\": \"Space Exploration Technologies ( SPCX +0.86% ) has been the most-talked-about stock of the year, and it's finally starting to take flight. It's only up 4% since its first-day closing price, but it's trading up 55% since it bottomed out in August.\\nIt has become the sixth most valuable company in the U.S., with a $2.2 trillion market cap, but Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) is keeping its top spot, getting closer to a $6 trillion market cap. If I had a choice of investing $10,000 in either one, here's what I would do.\\nThe case for SpaceX: Massive opportunities\\nIt hasn't paid to bet against Elon Musk in the past. Tesla has revolutionized the auto industry, and Tesla stock has gained 23,440% since going public in 2010. He has also been involved in several other major tech companies, including PayPal Holdings and OpenAI.\\nSpaceX is the culmination of many of Musk's innovations, bringing together the SpaceX rocket-launching business, the Starlink satellite broadband business, and the SpaceXAI artificial intelligence (AI) model. These are exciting innovations that are already in motion and making money, and the company sees a massive addressable market. It's planning to go to Mars, set up shop on the moon, and put data centers into space.\\nIn the company's first earnings report since going public, SpaceX demonstrated robust growth across its businesses. Total revenue increased 92% year over year, and net loss nearly halved to $541 million. The Starlink business reported $1.7 billion in operating income, a 79% increase over last year, and doubled subscribers to 12 million, while the rocket business remains the largest commercial rocket-launching business in the world . The two segments work together, since many of SpaceX's launches bring Starlink satellites into space. Starlink has more than 11,000 satellites in low Earth orbit and has plans for 100,000 in total to provide coverage to every spot on Earth.\\nSpaceX also plans to have 1 million Starmind AI satellites, which are mini data centers in space. Management sees its biggest opportunities in AI, and it has signed some important deals recently, including one with Anthropic, which will rent Nvidia chips from SpaceXAI's data centers, potentially worth $84.5 billion through 2029.\\nThe risks for SpaceX fall into two main camps. One is that there are many expectations, and the company may not meet them. The second is that the stock appears very expensive, trading at 69 times trailing 12-month sales.\\nThe case for Nvidia: It's the power behind AI\\nNvidia has skyrocketed as the power behind AI development. Its best-in-class graphics processing units (GPUs) have the necessary parallel processing power to drive generative AI, and it releases advanced platforms every year. This year, it has released the Vera Rubin series, which has \\\"10x agent throughput at scale compared with the previous-generation Nvidia Grace Blackwell platform,\\\" according to the company. The line includes a full computer system with several types of chips, including the central processing units (CPUs) that are critical components of agentic AI.\\nRevenue has recently been accelerating as the major hyperscalers ramp up spending. In the 2027 fiscal second quarter (ended July 26), revenue more than doubled to $96.2 billion, and gross margin improved from 72.4% to 75%. CFO Colette Kress has already given an outlook of 70% sales growth in 2028, although she said that without its supply constraints, sales would double.\\nNvidia has relationships with all kinds of companies in several mutually beneficial deals. For example, it has a $2 billion stake in cloud computing company CoreWeave , which in turn buys its chips, and SpaceX recently announced a deal to buy $40 billion worth of Nvidia chips for its data centers. Most well-known is probably its relationship with Amazon . Amazon offers Nvidia GPUs to its Amazon Web Services (AWS) cloud clients, and it just purchased another 2 million, while Nvidia uses AWS services.\\nAnalysts are predicting higher AI spend, and a nice piece of that goes to Nvidia. Goldman Sachs forecasts $1 trillion in global spend this year and frames its predictions as a percentage of U.S. gross domestic product (GDP). It predicts 1.8% of U.S. GDP in 2026, rising to 2.5% in 2027 and 2.8% in 2028.\\nOn top of all that, Nvidia stock looks cheap, trading at 30 times trailing-12-month earnings. The market is already pricing in next year's slowdown.\\nDon't worry, I'm not going to tell you to split it in half. I'm actually going to go with Nvidia. SpaceX could have massive opportunities, but I'm not convinced it's a buy at this price. It would also be good to see a few more quarters of growth data to better assess its risk.\\nThat leaves Nvidia , which has a proven track record and a highly profitable business at an attractive price.\\nBy Jennifer Saibil – Oct 11, 2026 at 1:18PM EST\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia released its Vera Rubin series in 2026 (\\\"this year\\\" in the 11 October 2026 article), a line that includes a full computer system with several types of chips, including central processing units; according to the company, Vera Rubin delivers 10x agent throughput at scale compared with the previous-generation Nvidia Grace Blackwell platform. The throughput comparison is the company's own claim, reported by fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-4\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue in its fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended 26 July 2026) more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, and gross margin improved to 75% from 72.4%, as reported by fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia CFO Colette Kress has given an outlook of 70% sales growth in 2028, and said that without its supply constraints sales would double, according to fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"SpaceX recently announced a deal to buy $40 billion worth of Nvidia chips for its data centers, according to fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Amazon, which offers Nvidia GPUs to its AWS cloud clients, has just purchased another 2 million of them, according to fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Goldman Sachs forecasts $1 trillion in global spend this year (2026) and frames its predictions as a share of U.S. GDP: 1.8% in 2026, rising to 2.5% in 2027 and 2.8% in 2028, as reported by fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-5\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"SpaceX has signed a deal with Anthropic, which will rent Nvidia chips from SpaceXAI's data centers, potentially worth $84.5 billion through 2029, according to fool.com.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"Will global AI spending equal 2.5% of U.S. GDP in 2027, as Goldman Sachs forecasts?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Global AI spending as a share of U.S. GDP in calendar 2027.\",\n      \"period\": \"2027 calendar year; check after 2027 U.S. GDP data are released\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported that in its fiscal 2027 second quarter ended July 26, revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion and gross margin improved from 72.4% to 75%.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia CFO Colette Kress has given an outlook of 70% sales growth in 2028; she said that without its supply constraints, sales would double.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"SpaceX recently announced a deal to buy $40 billion worth of Nvidia chips for its data centers.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"SpaceX signed a deal with Anthropic, which will rent Nvidia chips from SpaceXAI's data centers, potentially worth $84.5 billion through 2029.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"Amazon just purchased another 2 million Nvidia GPUs, after offering Nvidia GPUs to its AWS cloud clients, according to the article.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Goldman Sachs forecasts $1 trillion in global AI spending this year and predicts it will equal 1.8% of U.S. GDP in 2026, 2.5% in 2027 and 2.8% in 2028.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: B-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Nvidia released its Vera Rubin platform this year, a full computer system including CPUs.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"SpaceX plans to have 1 million Starmind AI satellites, which are described as mini data centers in space.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 11,\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Equity Investments Consume Half Its Free Cash Flow. Can the Payout Pace Last?\",\n  \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-10-12\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia passed our low-debt and strong-cash-flow screen. See the other businesses that cleared those financial hurdles alongside it . The chip business generates substantial cash, but shareholders also need to account for money invested in other companies. That spending sits outside the usual free-cash-flow calculation and can change how much remains for capital returns.\\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) reported August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year. Paid demand for its computing infrastructure supports the AI investment case. The narrower question is whether cash from that business covers both ecosystem investments and shareholder distributions.\\nThe investment bill sits below free cash flow\\nOver the first half of fiscal 2027, operating cash flow was $74.421 billion. Subtracting $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases plus $92 million of related principal payments leaves $69.895 billion of company-defined free cash flow. That is a useful cash measure, but it leaves out $42.404 billion spent purchasing equity securities. Sales of equity securities brought in $7.241 billion, making the net investment outflow $35.163 billion.\\nAfter that net investment spending, the same operating-cash base leaves $34.732 billion. Nvidia paid $39.044 billion for repurchases and $6.290 billion in dividends, a combined $45.334 billion. Net equity purchases consumed 50.3% of free cash flow. The resulting $10.602 billion gap required other funding on this narrow calculation. Debt issuance supplied $24.896 billion of cash; existing liquidity and other cash movements also matter. Our Check Point analysis examines when strong liquidity and a buyback program depend on financing beyond cash produced by the business .\\nThis bridge includes actual cash paid, not the entire value of Nvidia's investment portfolio. It also leaves acquisitions, employee-tax payments and other uses outside the calculation. The $99 billion equity-investment balance at July 26 measures an asset position; it is not a bill payable again next quarter.\\nNvidia also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26. Its August data-center guarantees carry a conditional aggregate cap of $105 billion, with exposure beginning as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029. Those future and contingent amounts belong outside the current cash bridge. Our earlier portfolio analysis follows the investment risks linking Nvidia, Intel and CoreWeave .\\nHolding equipment spending, net equity investment and distributions constant, operating cash would need to rise 14.2% to close the identified gap. Alternatively, net equity purchases would need to fall to $24.561 billion. These are funding thresholds, not forecasts. Investment sales and repurchases are discretionary, while commitments can constrain future flexibility.\\nThe bull case is that ecosystem investments broaden computing adoption and support future product demand. Nvidia's growing sales already provide a substantial cash engine. The bear case is that weaker customer funding or deployment delays could hurt both chip collections and the companies in its investment portfolio.\\nAt October 9 prices, Nvidia traded at 43.6 times reported trailing free cash flow. That denominator excludes equity purchases, so it cannot also be treated as cash entirely available for distributions. Insider Monkey counted 285 Nvidia holders in Q2 2026, up from 275 in Q1; Fisher increased its shares 3%. Those positions predate the August results. The next cash statement should show whether operating collections are keeping pace with the full capital-allocation program.\",\n  \"reads\": {\n   \"A\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [],\n    \"opened\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T1\",\n      \"question\": \"What will Nvidia report as its data-center guarantee exposure at its next annual report, given the disclosed $105 billion conditional aggregate cap?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The reported amount and any utilization of Nvidia's data-center guarantees at its next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal 2027 Form 10-K); the item says exposure begins as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-T2\",\n      \"question\": \"What will Nvidia report as outstanding equity-investment commitments at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of Nvidia's outstanding equity-investment commitments at its next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Nvidia's next annual report (fiscal 2027 Form 10-K).\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported on August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported first-half fiscal 2027 operating cash flow of $74.421 billion; after $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases and $92 million of related principal payments, company-defined free cash flow was $69.895 billion. It spent $42.404 billion purchasing equity securities and received $7.241 billion from sales, a net investment outflow of $35.163 billion. After that, $34.732 billion remained. Nvidia paid $39.044 billion for repurchases and $6.290 billion in dividends, a combined $45.334 billion. The article calculated that net equity purchases consumed 50.3% of free cash flow and that the resulting $10.602 billion gap required other funding; debt issuance supplied $24.896 billion of cash.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported a $99 billion equity-investment balance as of July 26; the article notes it measures an asset position and is not a bill payable again next quarter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments as of July 26.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T2\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's August data-center guarantees carry a conditional aggregate cap of $105 billion, with exposure beginning as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"opened here: A-T1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"A-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"The article calculated that, holding equipment spending, net equity investment and distributions constant, Nvidia's operating cash flow would need to rise 14.2% to close the identified gap, or net equity purchases would need to fall to $24.561 billion; it called these funding thresholds, not forecasts.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   },\n   \"B\": {\n    \"library_shown\": [\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n      \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\",\n      \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n      \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n      \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\",\n      \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n      \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\",\n      \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n     },\n     {\n      \"id\": \"clean-7\",\n      \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\",\n      \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\",\n      \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"\n     }\n    ],\n    \"opened\": [],\n    \"statements\": [\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S1\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA Corporation reported on August 26 that second-quarter revenue reached $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S2\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported operating cash flow of $74.421 billion for the first half of fiscal 2027; subtracting $4.434 billion of equipment and intangible-asset purchases plus $92 million of related principal payments left $69.895 billion of company-defined free cash flow.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S3\",\n      \"statement\": \"Over the first half of fiscal 2027, NVIDIA spent $42.404 billion purchasing equity securities and received $7.241 billion from sales of equity securities, making the net investment outflow $35.163 billion.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S4\",\n      \"statement\": \"After net equity investment spending, NVIDIA's H1 FY2027 operating-cash base left $34.732 billion; it paid $39.044 billion for repurchases and $6.290 billion in dividends, a combined $45.334 billion. Yahoo Finance calculated that net equity purchases consumed 50.3% of free cash flow, leaving a $10.602 billion gap that required other funding; debt issuance supplied $24.896 billion of cash, though existing liquidity and other cash movements also matter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S5\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported a $99 billion equity-investment balance at July 26; the article notes it measures an asset position and is not a bill payable again next quarter.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S6\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA also disclosed $25 billion of equity-investment commitments at July 26.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S7\",\n      \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's August data-center guarantees carry a conditional aggregate cap of $105 billion, with exposure beginning as facilities enter service, expected from fiscal 2029.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"library clean-1\"\n      ]\n     },\n     {\n      \"ref\": \"B-S8\",\n      \"statement\": \"Yahoo Finance calculated that, holding equipment spending, net equity investment and distributions constant, NVIDIA's operating cash would need to rise 14.2% to close the identified funding gap, or net equity purchases would need to fall to $24.561 billion; it called these funding thresholds, not forecasts.\",\n      \"filed_under\": [\n       \"nowhere\"\n      ]\n     }\n    ]\n   }\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\")); print(json.dumps(p[1:5],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\")); print(json.dumps(p[1:5],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"[{\"article\": 2, \"title\": \"3 Overlooked Growth Stocks Powering Every AI Chip Nvidia and AMD Build\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"day\": \"2026-10-06\", \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) are seeing booming demand for graphics processing units (GPUs) . The consensus estimate has both companies growing revenue roughly 90% and 45%, respectively, this year. But you don't have to choose between winners and losers in this GPU rivalry.\\nEvery chip still has to be designed, printed, and paired with fast memory before it can train or run an AI model. ASML ( ASML +1.86% ) , Synopsys ( SNPS +6.03% ) , and Micron Technology ( MU +4.25% ) provide these essential steps in the chipmaking process, offering investors another way to ride the growing demand across the chip industry.\\nASML supplies the extreme ultraviolet lithography systems that print circuit patterns on chips. Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing builds Nvidia and AMD GPUs, and it buys that equipment from ASML. Without those tools, the GPUs do not get made.\\nManagement sees surging demand for AI chips, prompting customers to add manufacturing capacity. Analysts expect full-year 2026 sales to grow 32% over 2025, then another 27% in 2027. Order momentum was \\\"extremely strong\\\" in the first half of 2026, driving backlog growth across a broad mix of customers.\\nThe shares trade at a price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio of 57 as of Oct. 1, 2026. That valuation leaves downside risk if growth stalls. However, CEO Christophe Fouquet said on the second-quarter earnings call that the company has demand visibility extending several years.\\nIn 2027 estimates, though, the multiple falls to 30 times earnings. Analysts expect 31% annualized earnings growth, a P/E-to-growth (PEG) ratio of about 1.0. That valuation leaves room for upside as the semiconductor industry continues to grow.\\nSynopsys is a leading supplier of electronic design automation (EDA), the software Nvidia and AMD use to design chips before being sent to TSMC. Growth is accelerating as AI and high-performance compute customers run more designs.\\nRevenue surged 42% year over year to nearly $2.5 billion in the fiscal third quarter of 2026. It converts that revenue into robust profits, earning an adjusted operating margin of 41.6%. A $10.9 billion backlog indicates more growth ahead.\\nAMD designed its Instinct MI4155X AI GPU with Synopsys' 3DIC Compiler. Synopsys offers innovation that sets it apart in a competitive industry. Its Multiphysics Fusion is the only product that integrates thermal analysis into the design flow, and Nvidia, Cisco Systems , MediaTek, and Samsung Foundry have validated it.\\nRevenue has grown steadily over the past 10 years, including during the 2022 chip industry downturn. Its consistent revenue growth reflects recurring software licenses that smooth out its revenue generation. The stock still looks like a solid buy, trading at a forward P/E of 28 on 2027 estimates, against expected annualized earnings growth of 22%.\\nMicron supplies DRAM and NAND flash memory for high-speed data retrieval and storage in AI data centers. Advanced versions are co-packaged with Nvidia and AMD GPUs, so a stronger GPU cycle pulls memory with it. Micron is also the only U.S.-based supplier of high bandwidth memory, which makes it a natural partner for both.\\nFiscal 2026 revenue hit a record $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year. Gross margin expanded 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a supply shortage lifted memory prices and profits.\\nManagement expects higher gross margins in fiscal 2027, though only a moderate rise in prices. Selling prices are the key risk. Long-term custom agreements account for over 35% of revenue through 2030, leaving a large share exposed to pricing swings.\\nManagement still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year. Micron is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E memory for next-generation GPUs.\\nOverbuilding supply to meet demand could eventually put pressure on memory prices. The prospect of another downturn in selling prices and earnings is why the market is pricing the stock at a conservative 7 times forward earnings. Still, there could be substantial upside if Micron can sign more customer agreements, thereby extending visibility into future revenue.\\nBy John Ballard – Oct 6, 2026 at 8:48AM EST\", \"reads\": {\"A\": {\"library_shown\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"}], \"opened\": [{\"ref\": \"A-T1\", \"question\": \"How much will high-bandwidth memory (HBM/DRAM) contract prices rise in calendar 2027 — the input price several AI-chip makers, Nvidia included, depend on?\", \"tracked\": \"The direction and size of HBM/DRAM contract price changes for calendar 2027 (e.g., TrendForce/DRAMeXchange contract price data and the pricing disclosed by Micron, SK Hynix and Samsung), versus Micron's stated expectation of only a moderate price rise in its fiscal 2027 and the shortage-driven price and margin expansion it reported for fiscal 2026.\", \"period\": \"Calendar 2027 (next calendar year after this 6 October 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a shared quantity; checkable in contract-price releases and memory makers' quarterly reports through 2027.\"}], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"A-S1\", \"statement\": \"Consensus estimates cited by the article have Nvidia growing revenue roughly 90% in 2026 and AMD about 45% ('this year'), per a 6 October 2026 article.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S2\", \"statement\": \"ASML management says surging AI-chip demand is prompting its customers to add manufacturing capacity, and that order momentum was 'extremely strong' in the first half of 2026, driving backlog growth across a broad mix of customers. ASML supplies the lithography equipment that TSMC, which builds Nvidia's GPUs, buys.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S3\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology reported fiscal 2026 revenue of a record $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year, with gross margin expanding 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a supply shortage lifted memory prices and profits.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S4\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology management guided fiscal 2027 to higher gross margins with only a moderate rise in selling prices, and still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S5\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology says it is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E memory for next-generation GPUs.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-4\"]}]}, \"B\": {\"library_shown\": [], \"opened\": [], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"B-S1\", \"statement\": \"The Motley Fool's John Ballard reports the consensus estimate has Nvidia's revenue growing roughly 90% and AMD's revenue roughly 45% this year (2026).\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S2\", \"statement\": \"Memory supplier Micron Technology reported record fiscal 2026 revenue of $133 billion, up 256% from the prior year, with gross margin expanding 40 percentage points to 81% for the year as a memory supply shortage lifted prices and profits.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S3\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology management said it expects higher gross margins in fiscal 2027 on only a moderate rise in selling prices, and still expects fiscal 2027 to be another record year.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S4\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology disclosed that long-term custom agreements account for over 35% of its revenue through 2030, leaving the remaining share of its memory revenue exposed to pricing swings.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S5\", \"statement\": \"Micron Technology is collaborating with Nvidia on the first custom HBM4E high-bandwidth memory for Nvidia's next-generation GPUs, according to the article.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}}}, {\"article\": 3, \"title\": \"Prediction: $500 a Month Invested in Nvidia Stock Will Be Worth This Much by Year-end 2030\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"day\": \"2026-10-06\", \"text\": \"Artificial intelligence (AI) has created some enormous multibaggers for investors, but none are quite on the same level as Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) . When ChatGPT helped ignite the generative AI boom in late 2022, Nvidia was worth less than $400 billion. Today, the chipmaker's market capitalization has ballooned to roughly $5.6 trillion -- making it the most valuable company in the world .\\nThis parabolic ascent shows how Nvidia evolved from a generic semiconductor company known for gaming GPUs into the undisputed king of AI infrastructure. Let's assess what might happen if you invested $500 in Nvidia every month from now through the end of 2030. Spoiler alert: Big gains could be in store.\\nHow big can Nvidia get?\\nBefore crunching any numbers, I need to come up with a baseline of where Nvidia might be headed over the next several years. The company generated $96.2 billion in revenue during its most recent quarter, representing a staggering 106% increase from the prior year. Even more impressive, Nvidia's data center business generated $89 billion, growing 117% year over year.\\nThe next chapter is already quietly unfolding. Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production. The company says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous generation Grace Blackwell architecture. This matters because AI spending is moving beyond simply training large language models (LLMs). Reasoning models, AI agents , robotics, and other inference workloads require enormous amounts of continuous computing power.\\nMeanwhile, infrastructure commitments from big tech are getting larger. The big five hyperscalers -- Amazon , Alphabet , Microsoft , Meta Platforms , and Oracle -- plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures (capex) in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027. Meanwhile, Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade.\\nNvidia is also expanding how it can monetize data centers. The company isn't merely selling GPUs anymore. AI factories now combine GPUs with other solutions from Nvidia, including CPUs, NVLink, networking, BlueField data processing units, storage infrastructure, and software platforms like CUDA .\\nI think complementing the legacy GPU business with adjacent solutions should help Nvidia expand its market share in the data center realm. Against this backdrop, I think the company can rise from a $5.6 trillion market value today to $15 trillion by 2030.\\nWhat investing $500 a month in Nvidia looks like\\nAn investor putting $500 into Nvidia every month would invest $6,000 over the course of a full year. Assuming the strategy begins in October 2026 and continues through December 2030, the contributions would total $25,500.\\nUnfortunately, calculating the ending value isn't as simple as multiplying $25,500 by Nvidia's expected return. The reason is because you will be using a dollar-cost averaging strategy over the lifetime of the investment. This means the initial $500 invested in October 2026 has more than four years to appreciate, while a $500 investment made near the end of 2030 has almost no time to compound.\\nIf I assume Nvidia's market capitalization rises gradually from $5.6 trillion to $15 trillion by the end of 2030, that works out to a 163% cumulative increase, or roughly 26% annualized or 1.9% per month when compounded. While this is a bit aggressive, I think it is also a reasonable upside scenario considering it's nowhere near the returns Nvidia generated during earlier stages of the AI boom.\\nIf I run the calculated return across all 51 monthly investments, the $25,500 investment could finish 2030 worth approximately $43,400. That represents roughly $17,900 in investment gains, or 70% upside.\\nObviously, no one knows what Nvidia will be worth four years from now. If the company reaches a $10 trillion valuation by the end of 2030, the same $500 per month strategy would grow to roughly $34,400. If the AI infrastructure supercycle sends Nvidia all the way to $20 trillion, the investment could approach $51,600 -- nearly a 100% gain.\\nThe first takeaway here is that Nvidia has enough structural tailwinds to support meaningful share price appreciation over the next several years. The second takeaway is to understand is that my estimates assume Nvidia's share price generally follows a smooth, compounded path toward the 2030 target.\\nIn reality, the stock's movement will almost certainly be messier. This means the price could rise or fall significantly from month to month. That's actually the entire point of dollar-cost averaging: rather than trying to time the market and predict exactly when Nvidia's stock will rise or fall, investors consistently put the same amount of money to work. For someone investing consistently every month, patience will ultimately matter just as much as finding the right opportunities.\\nBy Adam Spatacco – Oct 6, 2026 at 12:00PM EST\", \"reads\": {\"A\": {\"library_shown\": [], \"opened\": [{\"ref\": \"A-T1\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform — the data center product generation that carries the company's revenue — reach volume customer shipments, confirmed in reported results, by the check date?\", \"tracked\": \"Vera Rubin-based systems shipping in volume and contributing to reported revenue, as confirmed by Nvidia.\", \"period\": \"Check date: Nvidia's first quarterly results covering the quarter after the Oct 6, 2026 article (the item reports the platform moving into full production but gives no shipment date, so the check date is the next quarterly report following the item).\"}, {\"ref\": \"A-T2\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest hyperscalers (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle) spend on capital expenditures in aggregate in calendar 2026?\", \"tracked\": \"Combined calendar-2026 capital expenditures of the big five hyperscalers versus the approximately $800 billion figure cited in the item.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026; settled by the five companies' calendar-2026 full-year results (reported in early 2027), with quarterly capex disclosures as interim checks.\"}], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"A-S1\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported $96.2 billion of revenue in its most recent quarter, up 106% year over year, including $89 billion from its data center business, up 117% year over year, as cited by Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S2\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production, according to the article, which identifies it as the successor generation to the company's Grace Blackwell architecture.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S3\", \"statement\": \"Fool.com's Adam Spatacco states that the big five hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures in calendar 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T2\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S4\", \"statement\": \"Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade, according to Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}, \"B\": {\"library_shown\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"}], \"opened\": [], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"B-S1\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA reported revenue of $96.2 billion in its most recent quarter, a 106% increase from the prior year, with its data center business generating $89 billion, up 117% year over year, as cited by Fool.com's Adam Spatacco.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S2\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production, and NVIDIA says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous-generation Grace Blackwell architecture, according to Fool.com.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-4\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S3\", \"statement\": \"The big five hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027, Fool.com reports.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-5\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S4\", \"statement\": \"Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade, according to Fool.com.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}}}, {\"article\": 4, \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Forecast: Rubin Ramp and $108 Billion Q3 Outlook Keep AI Growth Accelerating\", \"publisher\": \"tradingkey.com\", \"day\": \"2026-10-06\", \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock Forecast: Rubin Ramp and $108 Billion Q3 Outlook Keep AI Growth Accelerating\\nNvidia achieved record financial performance in October 2026, driven by fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubling year-over-year to $96.2 billion, robust Data Center growth, and strong gross margins of 75.0%. Management projects Q3 revenue of approximately $108 billion, supported by the scaling of Blackwell Ultra and the full production ramp of Vera Rubin. Strategic initiatives include a $150 billion share buyback authorization and a proposed acquisition of Hugging Face. Key operational risks involve data center power constraints, customer financing challenges, and regulatory scrutiny. Technically, the stock remains bullish above the $233.17 support level, targeting a breakout past $242.15.\\nNvidia (NVDA) continues to reach record highs in October 2026. On October 5th, NVDA closed at $238.90, up 2.12% with an intraday high of $240.10. As a result, the market capitalization of NVDAis ~5.77T making it the most valuable publicly traded company.\\nWhile sentiment around NVDA has been quite bullish for quite some time, I do think the debate is beginning to shift. Investors are beginning to discuss the constraints that limit Nvidia’s growth, as opposed to discussing whether customers want NVDA’s accelerators. Blackwell Ultra is ramping, Vera Rubin is in full production, and data center revenue has more than doubled. The bigger constraint is whether customers can secure adequate power and financing, and data center space to deploy Nvidia’s accelerators.\\nNvidia Expands Buyback Capacity by $150 Billion\\nOn September 28, Nvidia disclosed that the board increased the company’s authorization for share repurchases by an additional $150 billion. Based on the SEC filing, “the repurchase of NVDA common stock shall occur from time to time in open market purchases, privately negotiated transactions or otherwise in compliance with all applicable securities laws and regulations”.\\nNvidia increased its share buyback authorization by $150 billion on September 28, the largest share repurchase authorization increase in history. Management currently expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028, and currently has about $235 billion remaining under the authorization.\\nThe buyback program demonstrates management’s confidence in the financial markets and Nvidia’s future cash generation potential to consistently execute its strategic imperatives and generate strong cash flow.\\nThe buyback program also creates confidence in management’s financial stewardship in that they expect to maintain product leadership and fund significant share repurchases going forward.\\nHugging Face Deal Expands Nvidia Beyond Hardware\\nOn September 3, 2026, Nvidia announced that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion. The agreement states that about $11.9 billion will be paid to the Hugging Face shareholders, and the remainder, up to $1 billion, will be given out as equity-based retention awards to employees in the form of incentive compensation. The closing of the transaction is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated to occur in the first half of 2027.\\nNvidia has stated that Hugging Face will continue to be open and interoperable with various models, frameworks and clouds. Hugging Face is already one of the most popular destinations for open AI models. For Nvidia, this means a stronger software moat, through CUDA, and an earlier position in the developer workflow.\\nThere are concerns, however, that through this acquisition, Nvidia will work to favor its chips, and deteriorate the relationships Hugging Face previously had with various clouds. Because of this and other concerns, this acquisition and many like it, will be under strong regulatory scrutiny. Although Nvidia will expand its software moat with this acquisition, it has created a similar challenge for itself in terms of regulation and other ecosystem management.\\nQ2 Revenue More Than Doubled\\nNvidia achieved fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, a YoY increase of 106% and an 18% increase from the prior quarter. Data Center revenue was $89 billion, a YoY increase of 117% and 18%. GAAP operating income increased by 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income increased 126% to $59.7 billion.\\nGAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS was $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, compared to 72.4% and 72.5% respectively in the prior year. The combination of triple-digit revenue growth and an increasing gross margin shows Nvidia is converting demand into exceptional profitability at scale, and shows the health of the business.\\nThat is the part of the story I find most impressive. Nvidia is not merely shipping more accelerators. Customers are moving toward Blackwell and Rubin type systems that bundle a variety of compute and networking components as well as software.\\nVera Rubin Is Ramping Into Full Production\\nBlackwell remains the primary source of revenue, but Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production. The company has racks deployed with partners like CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius. Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform are also arriving at large AI factories, Nvidia says.\\nThe annual product cadence is Nvidia’s biggest competitive edge. Customers deploying Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra are already preparing infrastructure for Rubin, which further decreases the risk of a long transition gap between product generations. It also pushes the technical bar higher for AMD and for custom accelerators built by hyperscalers.\\nNvidia is broadening its product portfolio beyond GPUs. The company unveiled a standalone AI CPU, called Vera, to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads. Like management expects, if Vera gains traction in the market, it could further expand Nvidia’s customer base beyond its existing GPU customer base.\\nThe $108 Billion Q3 Guide Sets Another High Bar\\nNvidia expects about $108B in revenue for fiscal Q3 2027 (plus or minus 2%). That represents a big jump from the $96.2B in revenue generated in Q2 2027. Management expects non-GAAP gross margins to be approximately 74% and GAAP gross margins to be about the same (plus or minus 50 basis points).\\nMost importantly, Nvidia's guidance assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China. Without the Chinese Data Center market, the guidance still seems very optimistic. China was one of Nvidia’s largest markets for accelerators prior to the export ban. Export restrictions mean Nvidia has very little access to sell its high-end accelerators to the market. So far, the constraint has not been a major issue because of the strong demand from U.S. hyperscalers, as well as, sovereign AI projects, startups, and enterprises.\\nThe Q3 guidance is now the most important near-term benchmark. An actual result above $108 billion with margins close to 74% would show Blackwell Ultra demand is very strong and Rubin's production ramp remains on track. A significant miss would be a warning sign because expectations are higher and the stock is trading at all-time highs.\\nAI Financing Is Becoming a New Systemic Risk\\nNvidia is partnering with major capital providers to establish independent financing platforms designed to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time. The partners include Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR. Nvidia states the purpose of these financing platforms is to provide funding for ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia.\\nAccording to Reuters, lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment due to the fact that advanced GPUs can quickly become obsolete as a new generation is released. Banks typically model the useful economic life of equipment at 3 to 4 years, while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer. If equipment is expected to be productive for a shorter period of time, lenders are unwilling to provide as much funding against the equipment, and may require other collateral.\\nThis is becoming a major concern because the AI boom is also becoming a capital boom. If developers and infrastructure companies are too reliant on debt to finance Nvidia systems, collections can slow even if the demand for compute remains.\\nPower May Become a Bigger Constraint Than Chip Demand\\nAccording to Morgan Stanley this week, Nvidia and Broadcom seem relatively protected from the current U.S. data center power shortage, but other constraints may affect broader deployment. Nvidia can still get orders even if customers don't have the means to build out the power infrastructure to support the order.\\nThe power issue is structural. Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta and Oracle, and other hyperscale/cloud companies are investing heavily to secure long term nuclear, natural gas and renewable energy sources. That's because the companies will need to build out new data centers and supporting infrastructure to meet customer demand. Nvidia's constrains are tied to how quickly the rest of the data center infrastructure ecosystem can be expanded.\\nIn my opinion, this is one of the most clear risks to the overall market and offers a fair valuation on Nvidia shares given the other constraining factors.\\nValuation Still Requires Near-Perfect Execution\\nAt the recent close, Nvidia’s valuation was approximately $5.77 trillion. Stock Analysis shows a trailing P/E ratio of close to 30 and forward P/E of 19. Nvidia’s valuation is showing extreme expectations for future growth and not reflective of the semiconductor industry.\\nInvestors have re-rated Nvidia as the core infrastructure and global platform for artificial intelligence (AI). Similar valuations to Nvidia’s current growth rates are not that extreme, but there is little room for slowdowns. Currently, Nvidia is experiencing exceptionally strong demand for its products. Nvidia must not only continue the strong demand for its products, but also manage the product transition from Blackwell to Rubin. Finally, they must maintain gross margins, and ensure customers can afford and have the infrastructure to support the systems they are ordering. Any major disruption to these areas could affect the positive sentiment around Nvidia.\\nNvidia Technical Analysis: NVDA Breaks $233.17 as Bulls Target $242.15\\nNvidia closed at $238.90 on October 5 after breaking the $233.17 resistance level and hitting an all-time high of $240.10 on the day. Nvidia is clearly above both of its moving averages and printing higher lows and higher highs. A bullish trend line from the July lows also supports the structure.\\nNvidia Stock Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\\nNVDA’s RSI is in overbought territory at 72 after touching 62 on its signal line. The overbought conditions and the approach toward the $242.15 resistance level increase the chances of minor consolidation before resuming the upside move.\\nThe upside momentum would require a 4-hour close above the $242.15 resistance level, with the next target at $248.82. Beyond that, the next target is $255.85. On the downside, a break below the $233.17 would be a bearish sign and the 50 period moving average at $225.01 would also provide support. The next major support after that would be the cluster between $222.01 and $220.21.\\nMy bullish outlook is based on Nvidia holding above $233.17. With the overbought conditions and RSI, I would expect a retrace before continuing higher.\\n- Latest completed close: $238.90\\n- Major support levels: $233.17, $225.01, then $222.01 to $220.21\\n- Major resistance levels: $242.15, $248.82, then $255.85\\n- RSI: approximately 72, overbought but bullish\\n- Breakout target: $248.82 above a confirmed $242.15 break\\nWhy is Nvidia stock in focus now?\\nNvidia’s current quarter is ablaze because fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubled, Data Center growth was above 100%, Vera Rubin is in full production, and management is guiding for another record quarter with $108 billion of revenue. Also, Nvidia is expanding its software ecosystem through the proposed Hugging Face acquisition while creating new ways to finance AI infrastructure.\\nWhat level confirms a stronger NVDA breakout?\\nA 4-hour close above $242.15 confirms a bullish extension, and a target of $248.82 becomes probable. A breakdown of $233.17 cancels the current NVDA breakout and increases the probability of a deeper retest to $225.01.\\nNvidia is entering October with perhaps the strongest Earnings potential in global technology. Q2 revenue was up 106%, the Data Center was up 117%, the gross margin was 75%, and management expects another record in Q3. Vera Rubin going into production, while Blackwell Ultra continues to scale, gives Nvidia the potential to keep its product lead.\\nThe risks now are more financing related. The availability of power for Data Centers, China restrictions, customer deployments, and the pace of AI rollouts will all play a big role in determining just how quickly Nvidia can meet customer demand. NVDA is still bullish above $233.17, however the overbought condition of the RSI on the 4-hour chart suggests a move to the downside and higher volatility in the short term is probable. The long term outlook of AI remains strong.\", \"reads\": {\"A\": {\"library_shown\": [], \"opened\": [{\"ref\": \"A-T1\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin (next-generation rack-scale AI) platform reach volume customer shipments and recognized revenue during fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Volume shipments and a first material revenue contribution from Vera Rubin-based systems, ramping alongside Blackwell Ultra, by the fiscal 2027 year-end.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); the item reports Rubin already ramping into full production with racks deployed at named cloud partners as of October 2026 and ties the fiscal Q3 2027 revenue guide to the ramp.\"}, {\"ref\": \"A-T2\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia complete its approximately $12.93 billion acquisition of Hugging Face by the anticipated first-half 2027 closing?\", \"tracked\": \"Closing of the announced Hugging Face acquisition after customary conditions and regulatory approvals.\", \"period\": \"First half of 2027, the closing window the item anticipates (calendar year).\"}, {\"ref\": \"A-T3\", \"question\": \"Will US export controls allow Nvidia to resume material data center accelerator sales in China during fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Any licence grant or easing of export restrictions that lets Nvidia record Data Center compute revenue from China, versus the zero-China assumption embedded in its guidance.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for this pending policy question; the item reports the export restriction in force.\"}], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"A-S1\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, with Data Center revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially. GAAP operating income rose 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income rose 126% to $59.7 billion; GAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, versus 72.4% GAAP and 72.5% non-GAAP in the prior year.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S2\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a step up from the $96.2 billion recorded in fiscal Q2 2027, with non-GAAP gross margin of approximately 74% and GAAP gross margin about the same, plus or minus 50 basis points. The guidance assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\", \"opened here: A-T3\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S3\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia disclosed on September 28 that its board increased the share repurchase authorization by an additional $150 billion, which the article calls the largest such increase in history; management expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028 and about $235 billion remains available under it.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S4\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion granted as equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T2\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S5\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia said Blackwell remains its primary revenue source while Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with the company's racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S6\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia unveiled a standalone AI CPU called Vera, intended to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads; the item presents it as a broadening of Nvidia's product portfolio beyond GPUs, without shipment dates or volumes.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S7\", \"statement\": \"Morgan Stanley said this week that Nvidia and Broadcom look relatively protected from the current U.S. data center power shortage, though other constraints may affect broader deployment, and that Nvidia can still take orders even if customers lack the means to build the power infrastructure to support them.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S8\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia said it is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms designed to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia stating the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than to shift the entire cost onto Nvidia.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S9\", \"statement\": \"According to Reuters, lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can quickly become obsolete as a new generation is released; banks typically model a 3 to 4 year useful economic life for the equipment while Nvidia says the life can be much longer, and shorter assumed lives lead lenders to lend less against the equipment and possibly require other collateral.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S10\", \"statement\": \"The item reports that China was one of Nvidia's largest accelerator markets before the export ban, that export restrictions leave Nvidia very little access to sell its high-end accelerators there, and that the constraint has so far not been a major issue because of demand from U.S. hyperscalers, sovereign AI projects, startups and enterprises.\", \"filed_under\": [\"opened here: A-T3\"]}]}, \"B\": {\"library_shown\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"}], \"opened\": [], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"B-S1\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; Data Center revenue was $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially. GAAP operating income rose 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income rose 126% to $59.7 billion; GAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS was $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, versus 72.4% GAAP and 72.5% non-GAAP in the prior year.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S2\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and non-GAAP gross margin to approximately 74%, with GAAP gross margin about the same plus or minus 50 basis points; the guidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-3\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S3\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia increased its share repurchase authorization by $150 billion on September 28, the largest such increase in history; management expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028, with about $235 billion remaining under the authorization.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S4\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion in equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-6\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S5\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-4\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S6\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms intended to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia saying the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S7\", \"statement\": \"Reuters reported that lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can become obsolete quickly; banks typically model a 3-to-4-year useful economic life while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer, which can reduce funding against the equipment and lead lenders to require other collateral.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-1\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S8\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia unveiled a standalone AI CPU called Vera to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads, saying it could further expand Nvidia's customer base beyond its existing GPU customers.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}}}, {\"article\": 5, \"title\": \"ASML vs. Nvidia: Which AI Semiconductor Stock Is a Better Buy in 2026?\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"day\": \"2026-10-06\", \"text\": \"As the artificial intelligence (AI) boom matures, investors can choose between companies that design AI chips and those that supply the specialized machinery required to manufacture them. Is ASML Holding ( ASML +1.86% ) or Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) the better play?\\nASML is the world's only manufacturer of extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography systems, the machines used to produce leading-edge chips. Nvidia operates at the other end of the supply chain, designing graphics processing units (GPUs) that train and run AI models in data centers. Both offer a way to invest in AI stocks , though they are growing at very different rates.\\nASML designs and manufactures the lithography systems that chipmakers use to print circuit patterns onto silicon wafers. It stands apart among semiconductor stocks because its EUV systems print the highly complex layers of advanced chips found in newer smartphones and AI hardware. The company sells to makers of both logic and memory chips, and its customer base is concentrated. Four customers accounted for about 61% of total net sales in 2025, according to its annual report.\\nASML reports its results in euros, and the figures here are converted to U.S. dollars. In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached about $37.1 billion, up roughly 15.6% from the prior fiscal year. Net income totaled about $10.9 billion for the period, with a net margin of about 29.4%.\\nOn its December 2025 balance sheet, prepared under U.S. GAAP, the debt-to-equity ratio was roughly 0.2x. That means total debt, including both short-term and long-term borrowings, was about a fifth of shareholder equity.\\nThe current ratio, which measures the ability to pay short-term obligations with short-term assets, was roughly 1.3x, and free cash flow reached close to $12.1 billion.\\nNvidia designs GPUs and the software that runs on them, which together are used to train and deploy AI models. In its annual report for fiscal 2026, which ended on Jan. 25, 2026, the company noted that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds risk, as losing one major buyer could hurt financial results.\\nIn fiscal 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. Net income followed suit, climbing to about $120.1 billion during the same period. That resulted in a net margin of about 55.6%, although net income included roughly $8.9 billion in net gains on equity securities.\\nOn its January 2026 balance sheet, the debt-to-equity ratio was about 0.05x on a total-debt basis, indicating limited reliance on borrowed capital relative to equity. Nvidia has borrowed more since then, and its July 26, 2026, balance sheet shows about $33.4 billion of debt, or roughly 0.15x equity.\\nThe current ratio at the end of fiscal 2026 was approximately 3.9x, indicating ample liquidity to cover near-term obligations. Free cash flow for fiscal 2026 was approximately $96.7 billion, representing the cash generated from operations minus capital expenditures.\\nASML depends on a narrow supply chain. It relies on external vendors for components in its systems, and each component is obtained from a single supplier or a limited number of suppliers, according to its annual report.\\nExport controls also limit which systems the company can ship to customers in China, which accounted for about 29% of total net sales in 2025. In addition, ASML is bringing its next-generation High NA EUV systems to market, and research and development costs ran about 14% of total net sales in 2025.\\nNvidia does not manufacture its own chips. It relies on foundries such as Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing ( TSM +2.34% ) to produce its wafers, and its annual report cites long manufacturing lead times and dependence on third-party suppliers as risks. Export controls have also been costly.\\nA U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and Nvidia said it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market as of the end of that fiscal year.\\nAdditionally, the company competes with large cloud companies such as Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) and Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) that design their own AI hardware.\\nNvidia trades at a lower forward price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple even though its price-to-sales (P/S) ratio is higher.\\nThe forward P/E compares the stock price to future earnings estimates, while the P/S ratio measures the stock price against sales over the past 12 months.\\nNvidia looks like the better buy to me in 2026, with a caveat that applies to both stocks. Each depends on the same wave of AI data center spending, so I'd own either only as part of a diversified portfolio held for five years or more.\\nNvidia is growing faster and trades at a lower multiple of expected earnings. Revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026.\\nManagement then guided for about $108 billion in the fiscal third quarter without assuming any data center compute revenue from China. On Sept. 28, the company added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization, which says a good deal about the cash this business generates.\\nThe risk is concentration. Two customers accounted for more than a third of fiscal 2026 revenue, and large cloud companies are designing their own AI chips.\\nASML deserves more than a passing mention. In July, management raised its 2026 sales outlook to a range roughly a third above its 2025 total, and it plans to add EUV capacity for 2027. Its machines are used to make leading-edge chips, regardless of who designs them, giving ASML a stake in AI's growth even if Nvidia's rivals gain ground.\\nThe shares have gained more than 80% over the past year, though, and the higher earnings multiple appears to reflect much of that strength. For investors who already own Nvidia, ASML still looks like a worthy long-term holding.\\nASML supplies the machines that advanced chipmaking depends on, while Nvidia generates nearly six times the revenue at close to double the net margin.\\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Oct 6, 2026 at 8:32AM EST\\nNvidia designs the GPUs used to train and run AI models, and its revenue grew roughly 65% in fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026.\\nTwo customers accounted for 36% of Nvidia's fiscal 2026 revenue, and four customers made up about 61% of ASML's 2025 sales.\", \"reads\": {\"A\": {\"library_shown\": [], \"opened\": [], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"A-S1\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia's annual report for fiscal 2026 disclosed that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively; the article also reports that the two customers combined for 36% of Nvidia's fiscal 2026 revenue.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S2\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2026 revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year, and net income of about $120.1 billion, a net margin of about 55.6%; net income included roughly $8.9 billion of net gains on equity securities.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S3\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia's revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S4\", \"statement\": \"Nvidia management guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to about $108 billion, without assuming any data center compute revenue from China.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S5\", \"statement\": \"On Sept. 28, 2026, Nvidia added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"A-S6\", \"statement\": \"A U.S. license requirement on Nvidia's H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and Nvidia said it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market as of the end of that fiscal year.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}, \"B\": {\"library_shown\": [{\"id\": \"clean-1\", \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-2\", \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\", \"tracked\": \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-3\", \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-4\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-5\", \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\", \"tracked\": \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-6\", \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}, {\"id\": \"clean-7\", \"question\": \"Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology formally approve purchases of Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU by Alibaba, ByteDance and other Chinese firms by the end of Nvidia's fiscal 2027, allowing those sales to proceed?\", \"tracked\": \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3, because the item gives no date of its own.\"}], \"opened\": [], \"statements\": [{\"ref\": \"B-S1\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 annual report figures, as cited by Fool.com: revenue of close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year; net income of about $120.1 billion; a net margin of about 55.6%; and net income that included roughly $8.9 billion of net gains on equity securities.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S2\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's balance sheet shifted toward more borrowing: the debt-to-equity ratio was about 0.05x on a total-debt basis at January 2026, and Fool.com reports NVIDIA has borrowed more since, with its July 26, 2026 balance sheet showing about $33.4 billion of debt, or roughly 0.15x equity.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S3\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026, according to Fool.com's recap of the reported results.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S4\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA management guided for about $108 billion of revenue in the fiscal third quarter, and the guidance does not assume any data center compute revenue from China.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-3\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S5\", \"statement\": \"On Sept. 28, 2026, NVIDIA added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization, a board decision Fool.com links to the company's cash generation.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S6\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA said a U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and that as of the end of that fiscal year it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market.\", \"filed_under\": [\"library clean-3\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S7\", \"statement\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal 2026 annual report disclosed that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively, i.e. about 36% combined.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}, {\"ref\": \"B-S8\", \"statement\": \"ASML management raised its 2026 sales outlook in July to a range roughly a third above its 2025 total and plans to add extreme-ultraviolet (EUV) lithography capacity for 2027, an upstream capacity signal for leading-edge chip output.\", \"filed_under\": [\"nowhere\"]}]}}}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[2:10]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"text\\\"]); \n for k,v in x[\\\"reads\\\"].items():\n  print(\\\"READ\\\",k,\\\"LIBRARY\\\",bool(v[\\\"library_shown\\\"]));\n  for t in v[\\\"opened\\\"]: print(\\\"OPEN\\\",t[\\\"ref\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"],t[\\\"tracked\\\"],t[\\\"period\\\"])\n  for s in v[\\\"statements\\\"]:\n   if any(z.startswith(\\\"library\\\") for z in s[\\\"filed_under\\\"]): print(\\\"FILE\\\",s[\\\"ref\\\"],s[\\\"statement\\\"],s[\\\"filed_under\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\"));\nfor x in p[2:10]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"text\\\"]); \n for k,v in x[\\\"reads\\\"].items():\n  print(\\\"READ\\\",k,\\\"LIBRARY\\\",bool(v[\\\"library_shown\\\"]));\n  for t in v[\\\"opened\\\"]: print(\\\"OPEN\\\",t[\\\"ref\\\"],t[\\\"question\\\"],t[\\\"tracked\\\"],t[\\\"period\\\"])\n  for s in v[\\\"statements\\\"]:\n   if any(z.startswith(\\\"library\\\") for z in s[\\\"filed_under\\\"]): print(\\\"FILE\\\",s[\\\"ref\\\"],s[\\\"statement\\\"],s[\\\"filed_under\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 3 Artificial intelligence (AI) has created some enormous multibaggers for investors, but none are quite on the same level as Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) . When ChatGPT helped ignite the generative AI boom in late 2022, Nvidia was worth less than $400 billion. Today, the chipmaker's market capitalization has ballooned to roughly $5.6 trillion -- making it the most valuable company in the world .\nThis parabolic ascent shows how Nvidia evolved from a generic semiconductor company known for gaming GPUs into the undisputed king of AI infrastructure. Let's assess what might happen if you invested $500 in Nvidia every month from now through the end of 2030. Spoiler alert: Big gains could be in store.\nHow big can Nvidia get?\nBefore crunching any numbers, I need to come up with a baseline of where Nvidia might be headed over the next several years. The company generated $96.2 billion in revenue during its most recent quarter, representing a staggering 106% increase from the prior year. Even more impressive, Nvidia's data center business generated $89 billion, growing 117% year over year.\nThe next chapter is already quietly unfolding. Nvidia's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production. The company says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous generation Grace Blackwell architecture. This matters because AI spending is moving beyond simply training large language models (LLMs). Reasoning models, AI agents , robotics, and other inference workloads require enormous amounts of continuous computing power.\nMeanwhile, infrastructure commitments from big tech are getting larger. The big five hyperscalers -- Amazon , Alphabet , Microsoft , Meta Platforms , and Oracle -- plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures (capex) in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027. Meanwhile, Anthropic has reportedly committed at least $518 billion to AI infrastructure over the next decade.\nNvidia is also expanding how it can monetize data centers. The company isn't merely selling GPUs anymore. AI factories now combine GPUs with other solutions from Nvidia, including CPUs, NVLink, networking, BlueField data processing units, storage infrastructure, and software platforms like CUDA .\nI think complementing the legacy GPU business with adjacent solutions should help Nvidia expand its market share in the data center realm. Against this backdrop, I think the company can rise from a $5.6 trillion market value today to $15 trillion by 2030.\nWhat investing $500 a month in Nvidia looks like\nAn investor putting $500 into Nvidia every month would invest $6,000 over the course of a full year. Assuming the strategy begins in October 2026 and continues through December 2030, the contributions would total $25,500.\nUnfortunately, calculating the ending value isn't as simple as multiplying $25,500 by Nvidia's expected return. The reason is because you will be using a dollar-cost averaging strategy over the lifetime of the investment. This means the initial $500 invested in October 2026 has more than four years to appreciate, while a $500 investment made near the end of 2030 has almost no time to compound.\nIf I assume Nvidia's market capitalization rises gradually from $5.6 trillion to $15 trillion by the end of 2030, that works out to a 163% cumulative increase, or roughly 26% annualized or 1.9% per month when compounded. While this is a bit aggressive, I think it is also a reasonable upside scenario considering it's nowhere near the returns Nvidia generated during earlier stages of the AI boom.\nIf I run the calculated return across all 51 monthly investments, the $25,500 investment could finish 2030 worth approximately $43,400. That represents roughly $17,900 in investment gains, or 70% upside.\nObviously, no one knows what Nvidia will be worth four years from now. If the company reaches a $10 trillion valuation by the end of 2030, the same $500 per month strategy would grow to roughly $34,400. If the AI infrastructure supercycle sends Nvidia all the way to $20 trillion, the investment could approach $51,600 -- nearly a 100% gain.\nThe first takeaway here is that Nvidia has enough structural tailwinds to support meaningful share price appreciation over the next several years. The second takeaway is to understand is that my estimates assume Nvidia's share price generally follows a smooth, compounded path toward the 2030 target.\nIn reality, the stock's movement will almost certainly be messier. This means the price could rise or fall significantly from month to month. That's actually the entire point of dollar-cost averaging: rather than trying to time the market and predict exactly when Nvidia's stock will rise or fall, investors consistently put the same amount of money to work. For someone investing consistently every month, patience will ultimately matter just as much as finding the right opportunities.\nBy Adam Spatacco – Oct 6, 2026 at 12:00PM EST\nREAD A LIBRARY False\nOPEN A-T1 Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin platform — the data center product generation that carries the company's revenue — reach volume customer shipments, confirmed in reported results, by the check date? Vera Rubin-based systems shipping in volume and contributing to reported revenue, as confirmed by Nvidia. Check date: Nvidia's first quarterly results covering the quarter after the Oct 6, 2026 article (the item reports the platform moving into full production but gives no shipment date, so the check date is the next quarterly report following the item).\nOPEN A-T2 How much will the five largest hyperscalers (Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle) spend on capital expenditures in aggregate in calendar 2026? Combined calendar-2026 capital expenditures of the big five hyperscalers versus the approximately $800 billion figure cited in the item. Calendar year 2026; settled by the five companies' calendar-2026 full-year results (reported in early 2027), with quarterly capex disclosures as interim checks.\nREAD B LIBRARY True\nFILE B-S2 NVIDIA's Vera Rubin architecture is moving into full production, and NVIDIA says the platform can deliver 10x the agentic AI throughput of its previous-generation Grace Blackwell architecture, according to Fool.com. ['library clean-4']\nFILE B-S3 The big five hyperscalers — Amazon, Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta Platforms and Oracle — plan to spend approximately $800 billion in capital expenditures in 2026 alone and roughly $1.3 trillion in 2027, Fool.com reports. ['library clean-5']\nARTICLE 4 Nvidia Stock Forecast: Rubin Ramp and $108 Billion Q3 Outlook Keep AI Growth Accelerating\nNvidia achieved record financial performance in October 2026, driven by fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubling year-over-year to $96.2 billion, robust Data Center growth, and strong gross margins of 75.0%. Management projects Q3 revenue of approximately $108 billion, supported by the scaling of Blackwell Ultra and the full production ramp of Vera Rubin. Strategic initiatives include a $150 billion share buyback authorization and a proposed acquisition of Hugging Face. Key operational risks involve data center power constraints, customer financing challenges, and regulatory scrutiny. Technically, the stock remains bullish above the $233.17 support level, targeting a breakout past $242.15.\nNvidia (NVDA) continues to reach record highs in October 2026. On October 5th, NVDA closed at $238.90, up 2.12% with an intraday high of $240.10. As a result, the market capitalization of NVDAis ~5.77T making it the most valuable publicly traded company.\nWhile sentiment around NVDA has been quite bullish for quite some time, I do think the debate is beginning to shift. Investors are beginning to discuss the constraints that limit Nvidia’s growth, as opposed to discussing whether customers want NVDA’s accelerators. Blackwell Ultra is ramping, Vera Rubin is in full production, and data center revenue has more than doubled. The bigger constraint is whether customers can secure adequate power and financing, and data center space to deploy Nvidia’s accelerators.\nNvidia Expands Buyback Capacity by $150 Billion\nOn September 28, Nvidia disclosed that the board increased the company’s authorization for share repurchases by an additional $150 billion. Based on the SEC filing, “the repurchase of NVDA common stock shall occur from time to time in open market purchases, privately negotiated transactions or otherwise in compliance with all applicable securities laws and regulations”.\nNvidia increased its share buyback authorization by $150 billion on September 28, the largest share repurchase authorization increase in history. Management currently expects the authorization to cover repurchases through fiscal year 2028, and currently has about $235 billion remaining under the authorization.\nThe buyback program demonstrates management’s confidence in the financial markets and Nvidia’s future cash generation potential to consistently execute its strategic imperatives and generate strong cash flow.\nThe buyback program also creates confidence in management’s financial stewardship in that they expect to maintain product leadership and fund significant share repurchases going forward.\nHugging Face Deal Expands Nvidia Beyond Hardware\nOn September 3, 2026, Nvidia announced that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion. The agreement states that about $11.9 billion will be paid to the Hugging Face shareholders, and the remainder, up to $1 billion, will be given out as equity-based retention awards to employees in the form of incentive compensation. The closing of the transaction is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated to occur in the first half of 2027.\nNvidia has stated that Hugging Face will continue to be open and interoperable with various models, frameworks and clouds. Hugging Face is already one of the most popular destinations for open AI models. For Nvidia, this means a stronger software moat, through CUDA, and an earlier position in the developer workflow.\nThere are concerns, however, that through this acquisition, Nvidia will work to favor its chips, and deteriorate the relationships Hugging Face previously had with various clouds. Because of this and other concerns, this acquisition and many like it, will be under strong regulatory scrutiny. Although Nvidia will expand its software moat with this acquisition, it has created a similar challenge for itself in terms of regulation and other ecosystem management.\nQ2 Revenue More Than Doubled\nNvidia achieved fiscal Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, a YoY increase of 106% and an 18% increase from the prior quarter. Data Center revenue was $89 billion, a YoY increase of 117% and 18%. GAAP operating income increased by 124% to $63.7 billion and GAAP net income increased 126% to $59.7 billion.\nGAAP diluted EPS was $2.46 and non-GAAP diluted EPS was $2.22. Gross margin was 75.0% on both a GAAP and non-GAAP basis, compared to 72.4% and 72.5% respectively in the prior year. The combination of triple-digit revenue growth and an increasing gross margin shows Nvidia is converting demand into exceptional profitability at scale, and shows the health of the business.\nThat is the part of the story I find most impressive. Nvidia is not merely shipping more accelerators. Customers are moving toward Blackwell and Rubin type systems that bundle a variety of compute and networking components as well as software.\nVera Rubin Is Ramping Into Full Production\nBlackwell remains the primary source of revenue, but Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production. The company has racks deployed with partners like CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius. Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform are also arriving at large AI factories, Nvidia says.\nThe annual product cadence is Nvidia’s biggest competitive edge. Customers deploying Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra are already preparing infrastructure for Rubin, which further decreases the risk of a long transition gap between product generations. It also pushes the technical bar higher for AMD and for custom accelerators built by hyperscalers.\nNvidia is broadening its product portfolio beyond GPUs. The company unveiled a standalone AI CPU, called Vera, to complement its accelerators for AI agent workloads. Like management expects, if Vera gains traction in the market, it could further expand Nvidia’s customer base beyond its existing GPU customer base.\nThe $108 Billion Q3 Guide Sets Another High Bar\nNvidia expects about $108B in revenue for fiscal Q3 2027 (plus or minus 2%). That represents a big jump from the $96.2B in revenue generated in Q2 2027. Management expects non-GAAP gross margins to be approximately 74% and GAAP gross margins to be about the same (plus or minus 50 basis points).\nMost importantly, Nvidia's guidance assumed no Data Center compute revenue from China. Without the Chinese Data Center market, the guidance still seems very optimistic. China was one of Nvidia’s largest markets for accelerators prior to the export ban. Export restrictions mean Nvidia has very little access to sell its high-end accelerators to the market. So far, the constraint has not been a major issue because of the strong demand from U.S. hyperscalers, as well as, sovereign AI projects, startups, and enterprises.\nThe Q3 guidance is now the most important near-term benchmark. An actual result above $108 billion with margins close to 74% would show Blackwell Ultra demand is very strong and Rubin's production ramp remains on track. A significant miss would be a warning sign because expectations are higher and the stock is trading at all-time highs.\nAI Financing Is Becoming a New Systemic Risk\nNvidia is partnering with major capital providers to establish independent financing platforms designed to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time. The partners include Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR. Nvidia states the purpose of these financing platforms is to provide funding for ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia.\nAccording to Reuters, lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment due to the fact that advanced GPUs can quickly become obsolete as a new generation is released. Banks typically model the useful economic life of equipment at 3 to 4 years, while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer. If equipment is expected to be productive for a shorter period of time, lenders are unwilling to provide as much funding against the equipment, and may require other collateral.\nThis is becoming a major concern because the AI boom is also becoming a capital boom. If developers and infrastructure companies are too reliant on debt to finance Nvidia systems, collections can slow even if the demand for compute remains.\nPower May Become a Bigger Constraint Than Chip Demand\nAccording to Morgan Stanley this week, Nvidia and Broadcom seem relatively protected from the current U.S. data center power shortage, but other constraints may affect broader deployment. Nvidia can still get orders even if customers don't have the means to build out the power infrastructure to support the order.\nThe power issue is structural. Alphabet, Microsoft, Meta and Oracle, and other hyperscale/cloud companies are investing heavily to secure long term nuclear, natural gas and renewable energy sources. That's because the companies will need to build out new data centers and supporting infrastructure to meet customer demand. Nvidia's constrains are tied to how quickly the rest of the data center infrastructure ecosystem can be expanded.\nIn my opinion, this is one of the most clear risks to the overall market and offers a fair valuation on Nvidia shares given the other constraining factors.\nValuation Still Requires Near-Perfect Execution\nAt the recent close, Nvidia’s valuation was approximately $5.77 trillion. Stock Analysis shows a trailing P/E ratio of close to 30 and forward P/E of 19. Nvidia’s valuation is showing extreme expectations for future growth and not reflective of the semiconductor industry.\nInvestors have re-rated Nvidia as the core infrastructure and global platform for artificial intelligence (AI). Similar valuations to Nvidia’s current growth rates are not that extreme, but there is little room for slowdowns. Currently, Nvidia is experiencing exceptionally strong demand for its products. Nvidia must not only continue the strong demand for its products, but also manage the product transition from Blackwell to Rubin. Finally, they must maintain gross margins, and ensure customers can afford and have the infrastructure to support the systems they are ordering. Any major disruption to these areas could affect the positive sentiment around Nvidia.\nNvidia Technical Analysis: NVDA Breaks $233.17 as Bulls Target $242.15\nNvidia closed at $238.90 on October 5 after breaking the $233.17 resistance level and hitting an all-time high of $240.10 on the day. Nvidia is clearly above both of its moving averages and printing higher lows and higher highs. A bullish trend line from the July lows also supports the structure.\nNvidia Stock Price Chart - Source: Tradingview\nNVDA’s RSI is in overbought territory at 72 after touching 62 on its signal line. The overbought conditions and the approach toward the $242.15 resistance level increase the chances of minor consolidation before resuming the upside move.\nThe upside momentum would require a 4-hour close above the $242.15 resistance level, with the next target at $248.82. Beyond that, the next target is $255.85. On the downside, a break below the $233.17 would be a bearish sign and the 50 period moving average at $225.01 would also provide support. The next major support after that would be the cluster between $222.01 and $220.21.\nMy bullish outlook is based on Nvidia holding above $233.17. With the overbought conditions and RSI, I would expect a retrace before continuing higher.\n- Latest completed close: $238.90\n- Major support levels: $233.17, $225.01, then $222.01 to $220.21\n- Major resistance levels: $242.15, $248.82, then $255.85\n- RSI: approximately 72, overbought but bullish\n- Breakout target: $248.82 above a confirmed $242.15 break\nWhy is Nvidia stock in focus now?\nNvidia’s current quarter is ablaze because fiscal Q2 revenue more than doubled, Data Center growth was above 100%, Vera Rubin is in full production, and management is guiding for another record quarter with $108 billion of revenue. Also, Nvidia is expanding its software ecosystem through the proposed Hugging Face acquisition while creating new ways to finance AI infrastructure.\nWhat level confirms a stronger NVDA breakout?\nA 4-hour close above $242.15 confirms a bullish extension, and a target of $248.82 becomes probable. A breakdown of $233.17 cancels the current NVDA breakout and increases the probability of a deeper retest to $225.01.\nNvidia is entering October with perhaps the strongest Earnings potential in global technology. Q2 revenue was up 106%, the Data Center was up 117%, the gross margin was 75%, and management expects another record in Q3. Vera Rubin going into production, while Blackwell Ultra continues to scale, gives Nvidia the potential to keep its product lead.\nThe risks now are more financing related. The availability of power for Data Centers, China restrictions, customer deployments, and the pace of AI rollouts will all play a big role in determining just how quickly Nvidia can meet customer demand. NVDA is still bullish above $233.17, however the overbought condition of the RSI on the 4-hour chart suggests a move to the downside and higher volatility in the short term is probable. The long term outlook of AI remains strong.\nREAD A LIBRARY False\nOPEN A-T1 Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin (next-generation rack-scale AI) platform reach volume customer shipments and recognized revenue during fiscal 2027? Volume shipments and a first material revenue contribution from Vera Rubin-based systems, ramping alongside Blackwell Ultra, by the fiscal 2027 year-end. Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); the item reports Rubin already ramping into full production with racks deployed at named cloud partners as of October 2026 and ties the fiscal Q3 2027 revenue guide to the ramp.\nOPEN A-T2 Will Nvidia complete its approximately $12.93 billion acquisition of Hugging Face by the anticipated first-half 2027 closing? Closing of the announced Hugging Face acquisition after customary conditions and regulatory approvals. First half of 2027, the closing window the item anticipates (calendar year).\nOPEN A-T3 Will US export controls allow Nvidia to resume material data center accelerator sales in China during fiscal 2027? Any licence grant or easing of export restrictions that lets Nvidia record Data Center compute revenue from China, versus the zero-China assumption embedded in its guidance. Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for this pending policy question; the item reports the export restriction in force.\nREAD B LIBRARY True\nFILE B-S2 Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and non-GAAP gross margin to approximately 74%, with GAAP gross margin about the same plus or minus 50 basis points; the guidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. ['library clean-3']\nFILE B-S4 Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion in equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027. ['library clean-6']\nFILE B-S5 Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories. ['library clean-4']\nFILE B-S6 Nvidia is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms intended to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia saying the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia. ['library clean-1']\nFILE B-S7 Reuters reported that lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can become obsolete quickly; banks typically model a 3-to-4-year useful economic life while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer, which can reduce funding against the equipment and lead lenders to require other collateral. ['library clean-1']\nARTICLE 5 As the artificial intelligence (AI) boom matures, investors can choose between companies that design AI chips and those that supply the specialized machinery required to manufacture them. Is ASML Holding ( ASML +1.86% ) or Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) the better play?\nASML is the world's only manufacturer of extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography systems, the machines used to produce leading-edge chips. Nvidia operates at the other end of the supply chain, designing graphics processing units (GPUs) that train and run AI models in data centers. Both offer a way to invest in AI stocks , though they are growing at very different rates.\nASML designs and manufactures the lithography systems that chipmakers use to print circuit patterns onto silicon wafers. It stands apart among semiconductor stocks because its EUV systems print the highly complex layers of advanced chips found in newer smartphones and AI hardware. The company sells to makers of both logic and memory chips, and its customer base is concentrated. Four customers accounted for about 61% of total net sales in 2025, according to its annual report.\nASML reports its results in euros, and the figures here are converted to U.S. dollars. In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached about $37.1 billion, up roughly 15.6% from the prior fiscal year. Net income totaled about $10.9 billion for the period, with a net margin of about 29.4%.\nOn its December 2025 balance sheet, prepared under U.S. GAAP, the debt-to-equity ratio was roughly 0.2x. That means total debt, including both short-term and long-term borrowings, was about a fifth of shareholder equity.\nThe current ratio, which measures the ability to pay short-term obligations with short-term assets, was roughly 1.3x, and free cash flow reached close to $12.1 billion.\nNvidia designs GPUs and the software that runs on them, which together are used to train and deploy AI models. In its annual report for fiscal 2026, which ended on Jan. 25, 2026, the company noted that two customers accounted for approximately 22% and 14% of total revenue, respectively. This level of customer concentration adds risk, as losing one major buyer could hurt financial results.\nIn fiscal 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. Net income followed suit, climbing to about $120.1 billion during the same period. That resulted in a net margin of about 55.6%, although net income included roughly $8.9 billion in net gains on equity securities.\nOn its January 2026 balance sheet, the debt-to-equity ratio was about 0.05x on a total-debt basis, indicating limited reliance on borrowed capital relative to equity. Nvidia has borrowed more since then, and its July 26, 2026, balance sheet shows about $33.4 billion of debt, or roughly 0.15x equity.\nThe current ratio at the end of fiscal 2026 was approximately 3.9x, indicating ample liquidity to cover near-term obligations. Free cash flow for fiscal 2026 was approximately $96.7 billion, representing the cash generated from operations minus capital expenditures.\nASML depends on a narrow supply chain. It relies on external vendors for components in its systems, and each component is obtained from a single supplier or a limited number of suppliers, according to its annual report.\nExport controls also limit which systems the company can ship to customers in China, which accounted for about 29% of total net sales in 2025. In addition, ASML is bringing its next-generation High NA EUV systems to market, and research and development costs ran about 14% of total net sales in 2025.\nNvidia does not manufacture its own chips. It relies on foundries such as Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing ( TSM +2.34% ) to produce its wafers, and its annual report cites long manufacturing lead times and dependence on third-party suppliers as risks. Export controls have also been costly.\nA U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and Nvidia said it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market as of the end of that fiscal year.\nAdditionally, the company competes with large cloud companies such as Alphabet ( GOOGL -0.61% ) ( GOOG -0.77% ) and Amazon ( AMZN +0.51% ) that design their own AI hardware.\nNvidia trades at a lower forward price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple even though its price-to-sales (P/S) ratio is higher.\nThe forward P/E compares the stock price to future earnings estimates, while the P/S ratio measures the stock price against sales over the past 12 months.\nNvidia looks like the better buy to me in 2026, with a caveat that applies to both stocks. Each depends on the same wave of AI data center spending, so I'd own either only as part of a diversified portfolio held for five years or more.\nNvidia is growing faster and trades at a lower multiple of expected earnings. Revenue more than doubled year over year in its fiscal 2027 second quarter, which ended July 26, 2026.\nManagement then guided for about $108 billion in the fiscal third quarter without assuming any data center compute revenue from China. On Sept. 28, the company added $150 billion to its share repurchase authorization, which says a good deal about the cash this business generates.\nThe risk is concentration. Two customers accounted for more than a third of fiscal 2026 revenue, and large cloud companies are designing their own AI chips.\nASML deserves more than a passing mention. In July, management raised its 2026 sales outlook to a range roughly a third above its 2025 total, and it plans to add EUV capacity for 2027. Its machines are used to make leading-edge chips, regardless of who designs them, giving ASML a stake in AI's growth even if Nvidia's rivals gain ground.\nThe shares have gained more than 80% over the past year, though, and the higher earnings multiple appears to reflect much of that strength. For investors who already own Nvidia, ASML still looks like a worthy long-term holding.\nASML supplies the machines that advanced chipmaking depends on, while Nvidia generates nearly six times the revenue at close to double the net margin.\nBy Mike Schwenk – Updated Oct 6, 2026 at 8:32AM EST\nNvidia designs the GPUs used to train and run AI models, and its revenue grew roughly 65% in fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026.\nTwo customers accounted for 36% of Nvidia's fiscal 2026 revenue, and four customers made up about 61% of ASML's 2025 sales.\nREAD A LIBRARY False\nREAD B LIBRARY True\nFILE B-S4 NVIDIA management guided for about $108 billion of revenue in the fiscal third quarter, and the guidance does not assume any data center compute revenue from China. ['library clean-3']\nFILE B-S6 NVIDIA said a U.S. license requirement on its H20 chips led to a $4.5 billion charge in the first quarter of fiscal 2026, and that as of the end of that fiscal year it was effectively foreclosed from China's data center compute market. ['library clean-3']\nARTICLE 6 Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) went nowhere for most of a year, then broke out in August and now trades at a record high. Ken Fisher's Fisher Asset Management is Nvidia's largest hedge fund holder as of the second quarter, with a stake worth about $18.2 billion, or 5.42% of its portfolio, after raising it by 3% in the second quarter. Nvidia ranks second in our list of his 10 best AI stocks. Click here to see the other nine picks .\nBulls say demand is still growing faster than Nvidia can build chips. AI is moving from training models to running them for businesses and consumers, and every new agent and app adds to the computing power needed. Weighing the AI chip giants? Read our comparison of Nvidia and Broadcom to see which is the better buy now . Management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply holds that number back. Its CEO has said growth would come close to 100% if Nvidia could make every chip customers want.\nNvidia is also tackling the worry that matters most to the market, which is money. It lined up large lenders, including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo, to finance customers' data centers, and it offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value. Its CUDA software lets the same chips handle both training and inference, which helps them keep their value, and its revenue per gigawatt of data center capacity has risen with each chip generation, according to its CEO. Even Stanley Druckenmiller has said he regrets selling Nvidia too early. Read what he said in our list of his 10 best AI stocks . Management also recently announced a record increase to its share buyback.\nMargins are the first worry. Nvidia cut its gross margin outlook for fiscal 2028 to 72% to 73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected. Its newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter, and industry reports say the ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. A delay would push sales out. Want more AI ideas? See our list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy before they explode .\nThe bigger risk sits with its customers. Big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter, their debt is rising, and Morgan Stanley estimates the cash they generate will cover only part of the data center spending planned through 2028. Nvidia is helping fill that gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand now depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. A default by a large customer such as OpenAI would test those guarantees.\nCompetition is also growing. AMD is pushing harder, and cloud companies are building their own chips with partners like Broadcom. A startup backed by former Intel CEO Pat Gelsinger is also going after Nvidia's AI networking business. See how it plans to challenge Nvidia's edge . That is the risk for investors in Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA).\nNvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) trades at a forward P/E of 25.7, only slightly above the sector median of 23.8 and about 39% below its own five-year average of 42.2. Analysts expect earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028, which puts the P/E at about 15 on fiscal 2028 earnings. The PEG ratio, which compares the P/E to growth, is 0.49 against 1.29 for the sector, so Nvidia costs less than half as much per point of growth as a typical stock in its sector. Analysts raised their fiscal 2028 estimate by about 24% over the past 90 days, and the stock looks undervalued for its growth, as long as Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) delivers on those forecasts.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nFILE A-S2 NVIDIA lined up large lenders including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo to finance customers' data centers and offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value; the article says NVIDIA is helping fill a customer funding gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. ['library clean-1']\nFILE A-S3 The article reports that big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter and their debt is rising; Morgan Stanley estimates their cash generation will cover only part of the data-center spending planned through 2028. ['library clean-5']\nFILE A-S5 The article expects NVIDIA's newest chip, Rubin, to make up only about 20% of data-center revenue in the current quarter. ['library clean-4']\nFILE A-S6 Industry reports, as cited by the article, say Rubin's ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. ['library clean-4']\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 How much credit support will Nvidia have extended to its customers' data-center financing — residual-value guarantees and similar backstops — as disclosed in its next annual report? The dollar amount of Nvidia's guarantees/financing commitments to customers (including loss coverage of up to 25% if chips lose value) disclosed in its next Form 10-K. Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; 10-K expected around February 2027) — the item describes the guarantee/financing support as already in place, so an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\nOPEN B-T2 Will Nvidia's newest data-center chip, Rubin, complete its volume ramp on the planned 2026 schedule, or slip into 2027 because of memory shortages? Whether Rubin's ramp stays in calendar 2026 or slips into 2027, as indicated by Rubin's share of Nvidia's data-center revenue and management commentary on supply. Through calendar 2027: whether the Rubin ramp holds its 2026 schedule or slips into 2027 (the slip window the item reports).\nARTICLE 7 NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s $20 billion deal for Groq's AI technology is facing a new legal challenge just as that technology begins contributing to Nvidia's push into inference.\nA lawsuit filed by two former Groq engineers in Delaware alleges that Groq's board improperly structured the 2025 transaction, which consisted of a $17 billion licensing agreement and a separate $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool for certain engineers who joined the chipmaker. The plaintiffs argue that the arrangement effectively transferred Groq's core technology and much of its engineering talent without treating the transaction as a conventional acquisition. Groq has called the lawsuit meritless.\nFor Nvidia investors, however, the bigger question is not simply whether the transaction faces litigation. It is whether Groq has become important enough to Nvidia's next phase of growth for that legal and regulatory uncertainty to matter. NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) also made our latest list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy in October , but another AI stock ranked even higher.\nGroq Is Already Moving From Licensing Deal to Product\nThat distinction matters because Groq is no longer a speculative technology bet.\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later this quarter. Nvidia said the system achieved 3,400 output tokens per second in an Artificial Analysis benchmark, delivering four times faster responsiveness than the nearest alternative platform.\nGroq technology is also part of Nvidia's broader Vera Rubin platform. Management estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt has expanded from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans GPUs, CPUs, networking, and Groq LPUs.\nHowever, Jensen Huang has indicated that Groq accelerators primarily target specialized, high-speed inference workloads, while Vera Rubin NVL72 remains the company's broader AI training and inference platform.\nThat makes the lawsuit more relevant than the $20 billion headline alone indicates. Nvidia is trying to defend its dominance as AI spending increasingly moves from training toward inference, where specialized architectures and custom silicon represent a more meaningful competitive threat.\nThat competitive shift also puts Nvidia's position against Broadcom under greater scrutiny, particularly as investors weigh which AI chip stock is better positioned as spending expands beyond traditional GPUs .\nPiper Sandler captured that opportunity in September when it initiated NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) at Overweight with a $300 price target, describing the company as the \"enterprise inference king.\"\nNvidia generated $96 billion of revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, more than double the prior-year level, while Data Center revenue reached $89 billion. Management expects approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says that outlook is supply-constrained.\nMorgan Stanley sees potential upside even to that forecast. The firm argued on October 2 that underlying demand could support growth closer to 100%, while Nvidia's broad customer base and ability to generate more tokens per gigawatt leave it well positioned as power and data-center capacity become the next AI bottlenecks. Morgan Stanley has an Overweight rating and $300 target.\nThose expectations explain why NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s valuation looks very different depending on how far forward investors look.\nThe same valuation question becomes even more pronounced when Nvidia is compared with another megacap technology leader, raising the question of whether NVDA or Apple offers the stronger valuation case at current expectations .\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) traded at a 24.88 times forward P/E as of October 6. Consensus estimates call for EPS growth of 95.23% in fiscal 2027 and another 69.66% in fiscal 2028, bringing estimated P/E down from 25.65 times fiscal 2027 earnings to just 15.12 times fiscal 2028 earnings.\nPut simply, Nvidia does not necessarily look expensive if those earnings materialize. The risk is that its valuation increasingly depends on maintaining extraordinary growth as inference becomes a larger part of the AI market.\nFor now, the Groq litigation does not appear large enough on its own to challenge Nvidia's earnings trajectory. But the dispute raises a broader issue.\nThe plaintiffs acknowledge that Delaware courts have not directly determined whether acqui-hire transactions like Groq's should face traditional M&A scrutiny. Separately, the U.S. Department of Justice was reportedly probing Nvidia's Groq transaction, while FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has said the agency would examine deal structures potentially designed to escape conventional merger review.\nThat matters because Nvidia did not merely obtain Groq technology. It also hired much of the engineering team behind it without buying the entire company. Greater scrutiny of that structure could affect how Nvidia and other technology companies acquire strategically important AI capabilities in the future.\nBefore the lawsuit emerged, hedge fund interest in Nvidia had increased. The number of hedge funds bullish on NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) increased from 275 to 285 in Q2, with Fisher Asset Management increasing its position 3%, AQR Capital Management 18%, and Arrowstreet Capital 10%.\nThe near-term test, therefore, is not the lawsuit itself. It is whether Groq 3 LPX scales commercially and helps Nvidia extend its dominance from AI training into inference. If it does, the $20 billion transaction becomes easier to justify against the earnings growth Nvidia is expected to deliver. If legal or regulatory scrutiny begins interfering with that commercialization, the Groq dispute becomes much more relevant to NVDA's valuation.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nOPEN A-T1 Will NVIDIA's Groq 3 LPX — its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology — reach volume customer shipments, and therefore first revenue recognition, by the time NVIDIA reports fiscal Q3 2027 results? Whether Groq 3 LPX moves from 'full production' to volume shipments in the quarter ending late October 2026, versus slipping, and whether the LPU line begins contributing revenue as management expects. NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026), checked at NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 results in November 2026 — the item states volume shipments are expected 'later this quarter' in an 8 October 2026 article, which fixes the check date.\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 Will Nvidia's Groq 3 LPX rack-scale LPU system reach volume shipments by the end of its fiscal Q3 2027? First volume shipments of Groq 3 LPX, Nvidia's first rack-scale LPU system built on Groq's high-interactivity technology, confirmed by Nvidia. Fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026) — the item's 'later this quarter' for volume shipments, with the item dated 8 October 2026.\nOPEN B-T2 Will the U.S. Department of Justice or FTC bring a formal enforcement action, or require changes to Nvidia's 2025 Groq licensing/acqui-hire transaction, by Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027? A formal DOJ/FTC challenge or settlement requiring restructuring of the $20 billion Groq transaction ($17 billion licensing agreement plus $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool). Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027 (late January 2027) — set by rule as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory matter, since the item gives no date for the probe or any decision.\nARTICLE 8 The demand has led to the blossoming of an industry that hyperscalers once had to themselves, including lucrative one-off arrangements and asset-light strategies.\nOver 300 neoclouds now rent GPUs to businesses, according to a review from researcher SemiAnalysis, up 55% in under 11 months.\nMarc Benioff, chief executive officer of Salesforce Inc., left, and Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., during the 2026 Dreamforce conference in San Francisco, California, US, on Tuesday, Sept. 15, 2026.\nNvidia GPUs are the most sought-after processors in AI, and they're in such demand that the chipmaker's stock climbed to yet another record this week, lifting its market cap close to $6 trillion.\nCustomers can now shop around for access to the chips at the giant clouds from Amazon, Microsoft and Google, as well as at so-called neoclouds like CoreWeave. They can also go to various online marketplaces or even buy the costly hardware directly.\nFor Nvidia, it all adds up to unrelenting growth, as management anticipates $108 billion in revenue for the October quarter, which would mark an 89% year-over-year jump.\nBut the paradox of choice can be a headache for companies needing computing power yesterday.\nWhile cloud infrastructure providers have ranked at the top of Nvidia's customer list for several years, the business is diversifying. Five clients accounted for at least 10% of Nvidia's accounts receivable in July quarter, up from three in January, according to a filing .\nIndustry research firm SemiAnalysis counted 323 Nvidia GPU providers as of September, up from 209 less than 11 months earlier.\n\"You're going to see a whole new crop of really, really exciting neoclouds with hundreds of billions of dollars backlog together,\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at a Goldman Sachs tech conference in San Francisco last month.\nHere's a rundown of the various options for accessing GPUs, and why each might make sense:\nMany big companies spend tens of millions of dollars per year on a smorgasbord of cloud services from Amazon, Google and Microsoft. Since the 2022 launch of ChatGPT, companies have increasingly turned to the hyperscalers for GPUs so they can run generative AI workloads.\nThe top cloud providers come with a reputation advantage. If a software company relies on Amazon and Microsoft for GPUs and other capabilities, it won't need to panic about prospective customers questioning its suppliers.\nMicrosoft Azure signage on the exhibition floor during the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, on March 18, 2026.\n\"When you're talking to enterprises, your subprocessor had better be Azure,\" said Bindu Reddy, CEO of AI assistant startup Abacus, referring to Microsoft's cloud infrastructure.\nIn the past year, leading AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed to spending over $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, which controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025, according to industry researcher Gartner.\n\"Hyperscalers are in a good position to show trust to the enterprises because of their 10-plus years of full-stack capabilities,\" said Gartner analyst Hardeep Singh. But hyperscalers don't always have as many GPUs as enterprises require, he said.\nAndy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, told analysts in July that the retailer and cloud pioneer won't be able to serve all the demand it foresees this year.\n\"I believe this dynamic will also be true in 2027,\" he said.\nIf the hyperscalers were adequate, neoclouds wouldn't be proliferating.\nModal, a startup operating virtual sandboxes where AI agents work independent of main IT environments, went from running on the hyperscalers to signing up with the major neoclouds, and now it uses 25 of them, said CEO Erik Bernhardsson.\n\"You can get a few hundred GPUs or maybe a thousand, but at our scale, we needed way more GPUs,\" he said.\nThe hyperscalers themselves are also chasing the neoclouds. Google and Microsoft have started tapping CoreWeave, even as they all compete with one another.\n\"Some of the hyperscalers have approached us about taking care of customers they're worried about because they don't have the ability to service those customers when they need it,\" said Marc Boroditsky, chief revenue officer of Nebius, a Netherlands-based neocloud with operations in the U.S.\nVideo generation startup Reactor uses GPUs through Nebius and hyperscalers, said CEO Alberto Taiuti. The location of data centers matters, he said, because Reactor wants user-created videos to show up right away. Nebius delivers the specific GPUs Reactor needs, solid customer service and sufficient hardware and software, at a good price, Taiuti said.\nMichael Intrator, co-founder and CEO of CoreWeave, speaks during an interview with CNBC on the floor at the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in New York City, U.S., Feb. 27, 2026.\nThe most visible neoclouds can require some upfront payment, and chips might not come online for months, because providers raise funding based on contracts and set up the data center equipment, Bernhardsson said.\nIt would be difficult for CoreWeave to turn over 10,000 GPUs to a new customer with one day's notice, said Chen Goldberg, an executive vice president. CoreWeave's near-term capacity remains essentially sold out, CEO Mike Intrator said on the company's August earnings call.\nIf companies want GPUs immediately, they might have to go beyond the name brands. Some neoclouds aren't household names because they target specific countries, which can be viable in certain cases.\n\"Capacity right now is tight, and your relationships with your suppliers is actually one of the most closely guarded secrets for companies like ours,\" said Zhen Lu, CEO of Runpod.\nSpecialist neoclouds can offer more flexibility than the larger GPU clouds, which often require upfront payments and long-term commitments. Some sell so-called bare-metal GPUs, which give customers more control but also require them to manage more of the technical work themselves.\nCompanies using these smaller neoclouds share the same concerns: When can they get the GPUs, and at what price? Sunny Smith, co-founder and technology chief at Massed Compute, said customers are often willing to commit to capacity when they expect prices to rise.\nOracle , one of the world's largest cloud providers, is letting clients bring in their own GPUs. The software maker has more debt than Amazon or Microsoft, and its credit rating is lower so it has less flexibility to go on a GPU spending spree. But it's happy to operate the technology.\n\"As we're generally able to preserve and improve margins in the case of things like bring-your-own-hardware, the ROIC for those types of structures will be even higher,\" Oracle CFO Hilary Maxson told analysts on a June earnings call, using the acronym for return on invested capital.\nOracle hasn't disclosed the names of companies that choose this path. John DiFucci, a Guggenheim Securities analyst who recommends buying Oracle shares, said it would make sense for Advanced Micro Devices and Nvidia, the top two producers of GPUs, to bring their own to Oracle.\nInstead of buying thousands of GPUs, early-stage startups with limited capital can borrow them for hours at a time for less through clouds. And for companies with heavy-duty computing needs, Oracle's new route might be more attractive than building whole data centers. OpenAI committed to spending over $300 billion with Oracle over five years, but it hasn't mentioned anything about bringing in GPUs.\nOpenAI declined to comment.\nThe method might make sense for companies that have the capital to purchase AI chips but don't have enough power, data center space or skilled labor. Like its hyperscaler peers, Oracle works hard to secure all three in healthy quantities.\nAnother emerging option is to cut large deals with firms that have truckloads of GPUs for rent.\nSpaceX arranged to turn over excess capacity in separate deals with hyperscaler Google and open-source startup Reflection .\nIn April, SpaceX agreed to provide Cursor with GPUs and then bought the AI coding startup outright for $60 billion. And in May, SpaceX landed a deal to rent GPUs to Anthropic for $1.25 billion each month through mid-2029. That's more than what most startups can afford.\nFor SpaceX, though, the numbers work out nicely.\n\"The current economics have translated into a less than one-year payback on our new capital deployments for compute,\" Bret Johnsen, the company's finance chief, told analysts in August.\nIt's not only SpaceX. In July CNBC reported that Meta was working to form a cloud unit that could sell AI computing power.\nMeanwhile, companies continue to install GPU-filled servers in on-premises data centers the old-school way as CEOs work to balance capability with cost control.\nRevenue nearly doubled in the enterprise and small and medium business parts of hardware maker Lenovo's Infrastructure Solutions Group during the June quarter. \"We're seeing more and more enterprises now starting to say, 'How do I bring AI into my four walls?'\" said Vlad Rozanovich, a senior vice president.\nThe hourly spot price for an Nvidia B200 GPU has more than doubled since March, according to data from Ornn, a startup that maintains indexes.\nCollaboration software maker Dropbox relies on GPUs in its data centers, CEO Ashraf Alkarmi said.\n\"If we want to do a lot more, I think our supply chain connections will still be beneficial and a structural advantage,\" he said.\nEverpure , which sells data center storage hardware and software, has acquired its own GPUs, to run open-weight AI models for the company's software engineers, said CEO Charlie Giancarlo.\n\"In a very dynamic pricing environment, it's always good to have multiple sources that you can go to,\" he said.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nOPEN A-T1 What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, compared with the more-than-doubling since March 2026 reported for the Ornn index? The end-2026 hourly spot rental rate for an Nvidia B200 GPU in the secondary/neocloud rental market, as tracked by Ornn's index, versus its level in March 2026 — a gauge of GPU scarcity, neocloud economics and hence the durability of demand for Nvidia data-center GPUs. End-December 2026 (calendar 2026, the 'this year' of this 10 October 2026 item); settled at the release of Ornn's B200 rental-price index for December 2026.\nFILE A-S3 Nvidia disclosed in a filing that five clients each accounted for at least 10% of its accounts receivable in the July quarter, up from three such clients in January — customer concentration in receivables increased over that period. ['library clean-1']\nFILE A-S5 AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed over the past year to spending more than $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, CNBC reported, citing Gartner for the fact that the two cloud providers controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025. The commitment figure is a multi-year contracted amount between those parties, not a Nvidia figure and not a 2026 data-center capex number. ['library clean-5']\nFILE A-S6 Amazon CEO Andy Jassy told analysts in July 2026 that Amazon will not be able to serve all the cloud demand it foresees that year, and said he believes the same dynamic will hold in 2027 — a claim that hyperscaler capacity will remain short of demand this year and next. ['library clean-5']\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, after the item reports it more than doubled between March and October 2026? The year-end 2026 level of the hourly spot rental price for an Nvidia B200 GPU, as tracked by Ornn's index. End of calendar 2026 (December 2026 reading); set from the item's reference to 2026 spot pricing and the year-end index reading.\nARTICLE 9 On October 10, 2026, NVIDIA Corp NVDA is reportedly in discussions to acquire the American startup Reflection AI, which specializes in developing open-weight models. This potential acquisition could deepen NVIDIA's investment in the artificial intelligence sector. While the specific terms of the deal are still unclear, Reflection AI was valued at $25 billion during a funding round in March.\nGF Value™ verdict: NVDA is currently priced at $229.28, which is 43.6% undervalued compared to its GF Value™ of $406.18.\nGF Score™: 95/100, indicating strong overall performance in various financial metrics.\nFinancial Strength: Ranked 9/10, reflecting a robust balance sheet.\nThe news of NVIDIA's potential acquisition of Reflection AI highlights the company's ongoing commitment to enhancing its capabilities in the rapidly evolving artificial intelligence sector. By acquiring a startup that specializes in open-weight models, NVIDIA aims to solidify its position as a leader in AI infrastructure and applications. This strategic move aligns with the company's broader goal of expanding its influence in the tech landscape, particularly as demand for AI solutions continues to surge.\nNVIDIA is recognized as the world's leading supplier of accelerated computing and AI infrastructure, with a market capitalization of approximately $5.53 trillion. The company designs graphics processing units (GPUs), data-center-scale systems, and networking solutions built around its CUDA software platform. Its offerings cater to various sectors, including gaming, professional visualization, and autonomous vehicles, making it a key player in the semiconductor industry.\nAccording to GuruFocus, NVIDIA's GF Value™ is calculated at $406.18, indicating that the stock is currently undervalued by approximately 43.6% at its current price of $229.28. This significant margin of safety suggests that investors may have an opportunity to acquire shares at a discount relative to the company's intrinsic value. The current price-to-earnings (P/E) ratio stands at 28.98, which is considerably lower than its 5-year median P/E of 55.91, further supporting the notion that NVDA may be undervalued. For more details, visit the GF Value™ page .\nThe GF Score™ is a comprehensive measure that evaluates a company's financial strength, profitability, growth potential, valuation, and momentum. NVIDIA's impressive GF Score™ of 95/100 reflects its strong performance across various metrics, particularly in profitability and growth, where it ranks 10/10. However, its valuation rank is lower at 4/10, indicating some caution regarding its current market price relative to its intrinsic value.\nNVIDIA's strengths lie in its exceptional profitability and growth metrics, which are crucial for long-term success. However, the lower valuation rank suggests that investors should exercise caution and consider the potential for a value trap, as indicated by the GF Valuation label.\nFor further insights, visit the NVDA stock page .\nCurrently, 33 gurus hold shares of NVIDIA, with 12 adding to their positions and 20 trimming their holdings in recent quarters. This mixed activity indicates a cautious sentiment among institutional investors. Additionally, insider activity has shown significant selling, with a total of approximately $968 million in insider sales over the past three months. This trend may warrant further investigation by potential investors.\nThe potential acquisition of Reflection AI could enhance NVIDIA's competitive edge in the AI sector, making it a compelling investment opportunity. However, with a GF Value™ indicating significant undervaluation alongside a cautionary valuation rank, investors should weigh the risks carefully. For those interested in NVIDIA's prospects, the NVDA stock page provides comprehensive data and analysis.\nNVIDIA's GF Score™ is 95/100, indicating strong overall financial performance across various metrics.\nNVIDIA is currently undervalued, with a GF Value™ of $406.18 compared to its market price of $229.28, representing a 43.6% discount.\nNVIDIA's current P/E ratio is 28.98, significantly lower than its 5-year median P/E of 55.91, suggesting it may be undervalued relative to its historical performance.\nREAD A LIBRARY False\nOPEN A-T1 Will NVIDIA sign a definitive agreement to acquire Reflection AI, the open-weight AI model developer reported to be valued at $25 billion in its March 2026 funding round? A definitive agreement (or the collapse of talks) for NVIDIA to acquire Reflection AI Check date 2028-04-10, set as 18 months from the item date of 2026-10-10 because the acquisition is not yet signed\nREAD B LIBRARY True\nOPEN B-T1 Will NVIDIA sign a definitive agreement and complete an acquisition of Reflection AI — the open-weight AI model startup it is reportedly in talks to buy, valued at $25 billion in a March 2026 funding round — by the check date? Whether the reported talks turn into a signed and closed transaction for Reflection AI (versus talks ending without a deal, or a deal repriced/delayed past the check date), as disclosed in NVIDIA's press releases, Form 8-K and quarterly filings. Check date 2028-04-10 (18 months after the 10 October 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet signed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\nARTICLE 10 Space Exploration Technologies ( SPCX +0.86% ) has been the most-talked-about stock of the year, and it's finally starting to take flight. It's only up 4% since its first-day closing price, but it's trading up 55% since it bottomed out in August.\nIt has become the sixth most valuable company in the U.S., with a $2.2 trillion market cap, but Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) is keeping its top spot, getting closer to a $6 trillion market cap. If I had a choice of investing $10,000 in either one, here's what I would do.\nThe case for SpaceX: Massive opportunities\nIt hasn't paid to bet against Elon Musk in the past. Tesla has revolutionized the auto industry, and Tesla stock has gained 23,440% since going public in 2010. He has also been involved in several other major tech companies, including PayPal Holdings and OpenAI.\nSpaceX is the culmination of many of Musk's innovations, bringing together the SpaceX rocket-launching business, the Starlink satellite broadband business, and the SpaceXAI artificial intelligence (AI) model. These are exciting innovations that are already in motion and making money, and the company sees a massive addressable market. It's planning to go to Mars, set up shop on the moon, and put data centers into space.\nIn the company's first earnings report since going public, SpaceX demonstrated robust growth across its businesses. Total revenue increased 92% year over year, and net loss nearly halved to $541 million. The Starlink business reported $1.7 billion in operating income, a 79% increase over last year, and doubled subscribers to 12 million, while the rocket business remains the largest commercial rocket-launching business in the world . The two segments work together, since many of SpaceX's launches bring Starlink satellites into space. Starlink has more than 11,000 satellites in low Earth orbit and has plans for 100,000 in total to provide coverage to every spot on Earth.\nSpaceX also plans to have 1 million Starmind AI satellites, which are mini data centers in space. Management sees its biggest opportunities in AI, and it has signed some important deals recently, including one with Anthropic, which will rent Nvidia chips from SpaceXAI's data centers, potentially worth $84.5 billion through 2029.\nThe risks for SpaceX fall into two main camps. One is that there are many expectations, and the company may not meet them. The second is that the stock appears very expensive, trading at 69 times trailing 12-month sales.\nThe case for Nvidia: It's the power behind AI\nNvidia has skyrocketed as the power behind AI development. Its best-in-class graphics processing units (GPUs) have the necessary parallel processing power to drive generative AI, and it releases advanced platforms every year. This year, it has released the Vera Rubin series, which has \"10x agent throughput at scale compared with the previous-generation Nvidia Grace Blackwell platform,\" according to the company. The line includes a full computer system with several types of chips, including the central processing units (CPUs) that are critical components of agentic AI.\nRevenue has recently been accelerating as the major hyperscalers ramp up spending. In the 2027 fiscal second quarter (ended July 26), revenue more than doubled to $96.2 billion, and gross margin improved from 72.4% to 75%. CFO Colette Kress has already given an outlook of 70% sales growth in 2028, although she said that without its supply constraints, sales would double.\nNvidia has relationships with all kinds of companies in several mutually beneficial deals. For example, it has a $2 billion stake in cloud computing company CoreWeave , which in turn buys its chips, and SpaceX recently announced a deal to buy $40 billion worth of Nvidia chips for its data centers. Most well-known is probably its relationship with Amazon . Amazon offers Nvidia GPUs to its Amazon Web Services (AWS) cloud clients, and it just purchased another 2 million, while Nvidia uses AWS services.\nAnalysts are predicting higher AI spend, and a nice piece of that goes to Nvidia. Goldman Sachs forecasts $1 trillion in global spend this year and frames its predictions as a percentage of U.S. gross domestic product (GDP). It predicts 1.8% of U.S. GDP in 2026, rising to 2.5% in 2027 and 2.8% in 2028.\nOn top of all that, Nvidia stock looks cheap, trading at 30 times trailing-12-month earnings. The market is already pricing in next year's slowdown.\nDon't worry, I'm not going to tell you to split it in half. I'm actually going to go with Nvidia. SpaceX could have massive opportunities, but I'm not convinced it's a buy at this price. It would also be good to see a few more quarters of growth data to better assess its risk.\nThat leaves Nvidia , which has a proven track record and a highly profitable business at an attractive price.\nBy Jennifer Saibil – Oct 11, 2026 at 1:18PM EST\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nFILE A-S1 Nvidia released its Vera Rubin series in 2026 (\"this year\" in the 11 October 2026 article), a line that includes a full computer system with several types of chips, including central processing units; according to the company, Vera Rubin delivers 10x agent throughput at scale compared with the previous-generation Nvidia Grace Blackwell platform. The throughput comparison is the company's own claim, reported by fool.com. ['library clean-4']\nFILE A-S3 Nvidia CFO Colette Kress has given an outlook of 70% sales growth in 2028, and said that without its supply constraints sales would double, according to fool.com. ['library clean-2']\nFILE A-S5 Amazon, which offers Nvidia GPUs to its AWS cloud clients, has just purchased another 2 million of them, according to fool.com. ['library clean-5']\nFILE A-S6 Goldman Sachs forecasts $1 trillion in global spend this year (2026) and frames its predictions as a share of U.S. GDP: 1.8% in 2026, rising to 2.5% in 2027 and 2.8% in 2028, as reported by fool.com. ['library clean-5']\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 Will global AI spending equal 2.5% of U.S. GDP in 2027, as Goldman Sachs forecasts? Global AI spending as a share of U.S. GDP in calendar 2027. 2027 calendar year; check after 2027 U.S. GDP data are released\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\"));\nfor x in [p[i-1] for i in [4,6,7,8]]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"text\\\"] if x[\\\"article\\\"]\"'!=4 else \"already read\");\n for k,v in x[\"reads\"].items():\n  print(\"READ\",k,\"LIBRARY\",bool(v[\"library_shown\"]));\n  for t in v[\"opened\"]: print(\"OPEN\",t[\"ref\"],t[\"question\"],t[\"tracked\"],t[\"period\"])\n  for s in v[\"statements\"]:\n   if any(z.startswith(\"library\") for z in s[\"filed_under\"]): print(\"FILE\",s[\"ref\"],s[\"statement\"],s[\"filed_under\"])'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-library/reader-packet.json\\\"));\nfor x in [p[i-1] for i in [4,6,7,8]]:\n print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"text\\\"] if x[\\\"article\\\"]\"'!=4 else \"already read\");\n for k,v in x[\"reads\"].items():\n  print(\"READ\",k,\"LIBRARY\",bool(v[\"library_shown\"]));\n  for t in v[\"opened\"]: print(\"OPEN\",t[\"ref\"],t[\"question\"],t[\"tracked\"],t[\"period\"])\n  for s in v[\"statements\"]:\n   if any(z.startswith(\"library\") for z in s[\"filed_under\"]): print(\"FILE\",s[\"ref\"],s[\"statement\"],s[\"filed_under\"])'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 4 already read\nREAD A LIBRARY False\nOPEN A-T1 Will Nvidia's Vera Rubin (next-generation rack-scale AI) platform reach volume customer shipments and recognized revenue during fiscal 2027? Volume shipments and a first material revenue contribution from Vera Rubin-based systems, ramping alongside Blackwell Ultra, by the fiscal 2027 year-end. Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027); the item reports Rubin already ramping into full production with racks deployed at named cloud partners as of October 2026 and ties the fiscal Q3 2027 revenue guide to the ramp.\nOPEN A-T2 Will Nvidia complete its approximately $12.93 billion acquisition of Hugging Face by the anticipated first-half 2027 closing? Closing of the announced Hugging Face acquisition after customary conditions and regulatory approvals. First half of 2027, the closing window the item anticipates (calendar year).\nOPEN A-T3 Will US export controls allow Nvidia to resume material data center accelerator sales in China during fiscal 2027? Any licence grant or easing of export restrictions that lets Nvidia record Data Center compute revenue from China, versus the zero-China assumption embedded in its guidance. Fiscal 2027 (year ending late January 2027), set as the current fiscal year-end for this pending policy question; the item reports the export restriction in force.\nREAD B LIBRARY True\nFILE B-S2 Nvidia guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to about $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and non-GAAP gross margin to approximately 74%, with GAAP gross margin about the same plus or minus 50 basis points; the guidance assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. ['library clean-3']\nFILE B-S4 Nvidia announced on September 3, 2026 that it signed an agreement to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.93 billion, with about $11.9 billion paid to Hugging Face shareholders and up to $1 billion in equity-based retention awards to employees; closing is subject to customary conditions and regulatory approvals and is anticipated in the first half of 2027. ['library clean-6']\nFILE B-S5 Nvidia says Vera Rubin is already ramping into full production, with racks deployed at partners including CoreWeave, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure and Nebius, and Spectrum-6 networking systems designed for the Rubin platform arriving at large AI factories. ['library clean-4']\nFILE B-S6 Nvidia is partnering with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to establish independent financing platforms intended to mobilize over $500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time, with Nvidia saying the purpose is to fund ecosystem partners and customers rather than shifting the entire cost to Nvidia. ['library clean-1']\nFILE B-S7 Reuters reported that lenders are cautious on structures backed by AI computing equipment because advanced GPUs can become obsolete quickly; banks typically model a 3-to-4-year useful economic life while Nvidia says the useful life can be much longer, which can reduce funding against the equipment and lead lenders to require other collateral. ['library clean-1']\nARTICLE 6 Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) went nowhere for most of a year, then broke out in August and now trades at a record high. Ken Fisher's Fisher Asset Management is Nvidia's largest hedge fund holder as of the second quarter, with a stake worth about $18.2 billion, or 5.42% of its portfolio, after raising it by 3% in the second quarter. Nvidia ranks second in our list of his 10 best AI stocks. Click here to see the other nine picks .\nBulls say demand is still growing faster than Nvidia can build chips. AI is moving from training models to running them for businesses and consumers, and every new agent and app adds to the computing power needed. Weighing the AI chip giants? Read our comparison of Nvidia and Broadcom to see which is the better buy now . Management expects about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says supply holds that number back. Its CEO has said growth would come close to 100% if Nvidia could make every chip customers want.\nNvidia is also tackling the worry that matters most to the market, which is money. It lined up large lenders, including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo, to finance customers' data centers, and it offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value. Its CUDA software lets the same chips handle both training and inference, which helps them keep their value, and its revenue per gigawatt of data center capacity has risen with each chip generation, according to its CEO. Even Stanley Druckenmiller has said he regrets selling Nvidia too early. Read what he said in our list of his 10 best AI stocks . Management also recently announced a record increase to its share buyback.\nMargins are the first worry. Nvidia cut its gross margin outlook for fiscal 2028 to 72% to 73%, down from about 75%, because memory prices have risen faster than expected. Its newest chip, Rubin, should make up only about 20% of data center revenue this quarter, and industry reports say the ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. A delay would push sales out. Want more AI ideas? See our list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy before they explode .\nThe bigger risk sits with its customers. Big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter, their debt is rising, and Morgan Stanley estimates the cash they generate will cover only part of the data center spending planned through 2028. Nvidia is helping fill that gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand now depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. A default by a large customer such as OpenAI would test those guarantees.\nCompetition is also growing. AMD is pushing harder, and cloud companies are building their own chips with partners like Broadcom. A startup backed by former Intel CEO Pat Gelsinger is also going after Nvidia's AI networking business. See how it plans to challenge Nvidia's edge . That is the risk for investors in Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA).\nNvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) trades at a forward P/E of 25.7, only slightly above the sector median of 23.8 and about 39% below its own five-year average of 42.2. Analysts expect earnings per share to rise 95% in fiscal 2027 and 70% in fiscal 2028, which puts the P/E at about 15 on fiscal 2028 earnings. The PEG ratio, which compares the P/E to growth, is 0.49 against 1.29 for the sector, so Nvidia costs less than half as much per point of growth as a typical stock in its sector. Analysts raised their fiscal 2028 estimate by about 24% over the past 90 days, and the stock looks undervalued for its growth, as long as Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) delivers on those forecasts.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nFILE A-S2 NVIDIA lined up large lenders including Goldman Sachs, Blackstone and Apollo to finance customers' data centers and offers to cover up to 25% of the loss if the chips lose value; the article says NVIDIA is helping fill a customer funding gap with guarantees and financing support, so part of its demand depends on borrowing staying affordable while rates climb. ['library clean-1']\nFILE A-S3 The article reports that big cloud companies burned cash in the second quarter and their debt is rising; Morgan Stanley estimates their cash generation will cover only part of the data-center spending planned through 2028. ['library clean-5']\nFILE A-S5 The article expects NVIDIA's newest chip, Rubin, to make up only about 20% of data-center revenue in the current quarter. ['library clean-4']\nFILE A-S6 Industry reports, as cited by the article, say Rubin's ramp could slip into 2027 because of memory shortages. ['library clean-4']\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 How much credit support will Nvidia have extended to its customers' data-center financing — residual-value guarantees and similar backstops — as disclosed in its next annual report? The dollar amount of Nvidia's guarantees/financing commitments to customers (including loss coverage of up to 25% if chips lose value) disclosed in its next Form 10-K. Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; 10-K expected around February 2027) — the item describes the guarantee/financing support as already in place, so an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report.\nOPEN B-T2 Will Nvidia's newest data-center chip, Rubin, complete its volume ramp on the planned 2026 schedule, or slip into 2027 because of memory shortages? Whether Rubin's ramp stays in calendar 2026 or slips into 2027, as indicated by Rubin's share of Nvidia's data-center revenue and management commentary on supply. Through calendar 2027: whether the Rubin ramp holds its 2026 schedule or slips into 2027 (the slip window the item reports).\nARTICLE 7 NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s $20 billion deal for Groq's AI technology is facing a new legal challenge just as that technology begins contributing to Nvidia's push into inference.\nA lawsuit filed by two former Groq engineers in Delaware alleges that Groq's board improperly structured the 2025 transaction, which consisted of a $17 billion licensing agreement and a separate $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool for certain engineers who joined the chipmaker. The plaintiffs argue that the arrangement effectively transferred Groq's core technology and much of its engineering talent without treating the transaction as a conventional acquisition. Groq has called the lawsuit meritless.\nFor Nvidia investors, however, the bigger question is not simply whether the transaction faces litigation. It is whether Groq has become important enough to Nvidia's next phase of growth for that legal and regulatory uncertainty to matter. NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) also made our latest list of the 10 best AI stocks to buy in October , but another AI stock ranked even higher.\nGroq Is Already Moving From Licensing Deal to Product\nThat distinction matters because Groq is no longer a speculative technology bet.\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) said Groq 3 LPX, its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology, is already in full production, with volume shipments expected later this quarter. Nvidia said the system achieved 3,400 output tokens per second in an Artificial Analysis benchmark, delivering four times faster responsiveness than the nearest alternative platform.\nGroq technology is also part of Nvidia's broader Vera Rubin platform. Management estimates that its revenue opportunity per gigawatt has expanded from roughly $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin, which now spans GPUs, CPUs, networking, and Groq LPUs.\nHowever, Jensen Huang has indicated that Groq accelerators primarily target specialized, high-speed inference workloads, while Vera Rubin NVL72 remains the company's broader AI training and inference platform.\nThat makes the lawsuit more relevant than the $20 billion headline alone indicates. Nvidia is trying to defend its dominance as AI spending increasingly moves from training toward inference, where specialized architectures and custom silicon represent a more meaningful competitive threat.\nThat competitive shift also puts Nvidia's position against Broadcom under greater scrutiny, particularly as investors weigh which AI chip stock is better positioned as spending expands beyond traditional GPUs .\nPiper Sandler captured that opportunity in September when it initiated NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) at Overweight with a $300 price target, describing the company as the \"enterprise inference king.\"\nNvidia generated $96 billion of revenue in fiscal Q2 2027, more than double the prior-year level, while Data Center revenue reached $89 billion. Management expects approximately 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 and says that outlook is supply-constrained.\nMorgan Stanley sees potential upside even to that forecast. The firm argued on October 2 that underlying demand could support growth closer to 100%, while Nvidia's broad customer base and ability to generate more tokens per gigawatt leave it well positioned as power and data-center capacity become the next AI bottlenecks. Morgan Stanley has an Overweight rating and $300 target.\nThose expectations explain why NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA)'s valuation looks very different depending on how far forward investors look.\nThe same valuation question becomes even more pronounced when Nvidia is compared with another megacap technology leader, raising the question of whether NVDA or Apple offers the stronger valuation case at current expectations .\nNVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) traded at a 24.88 times forward P/E as of October 6. Consensus estimates call for EPS growth of 95.23% in fiscal 2027 and another 69.66% in fiscal 2028, bringing estimated P/E down from 25.65 times fiscal 2027 earnings to just 15.12 times fiscal 2028 earnings.\nPut simply, Nvidia does not necessarily look expensive if those earnings materialize. The risk is that its valuation increasingly depends on maintaining extraordinary growth as inference becomes a larger part of the AI market.\nFor now, the Groq litigation does not appear large enough on its own to challenge Nvidia's earnings trajectory. But the dispute raises a broader issue.\nThe plaintiffs acknowledge that Delaware courts have not directly determined whether acqui-hire transactions like Groq's should face traditional M&A scrutiny. Separately, the U.S. Department of Justice was reportedly probing Nvidia's Groq transaction, while FTC Chair Andrew Ferguson has said the agency would examine deal structures potentially designed to escape conventional merger review.\nThat matters because Nvidia did not merely obtain Groq technology. It also hired much of the engineering team behind it without buying the entire company. Greater scrutiny of that structure could affect how Nvidia and other technology companies acquire strategically important AI capabilities in the future.\nBefore the lawsuit emerged, hedge fund interest in Nvidia had increased. The number of hedge funds bullish on NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) increased from 275 to 285 in Q2, with Fisher Asset Management increasing its position 3%, AQR Capital Management 18%, and Arrowstreet Capital 10%.\nThe near-term test, therefore, is not the lawsuit itself. It is whether Groq 3 LPX scales commercially and helps Nvidia extend its dominance from AI training into inference. If it does, the $20 billion transaction becomes easier to justify against the earnings growth Nvidia is expected to deliver. If legal or regulatory scrutiny begins interfering with that commercialization, the Groq dispute becomes much more relevant to NVDA's valuation.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nOPEN A-T1 Will NVIDIA's Groq 3 LPX — its first rack-scale LPU system combining Nvidia's high-throughput architecture with Groq's high-interactivity technology — reach volume customer shipments, and therefore first revenue recognition, by the time NVIDIA reports fiscal Q3 2027 results? Whether Groq 3 LPX moves from 'full production' to volume shipments in the quarter ending late October 2026, versus slipping, and whether the LPU line begins contributing revenue as management expects. NVIDIA fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026), checked at NVIDIA's Q3 FY2027 results in November 2026 — the item states volume shipments are expected 'later this quarter' in an 8 October 2026 article, which fixes the check date.\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 Will Nvidia's Groq 3 LPX rack-scale LPU system reach volume shipments by the end of its fiscal Q3 2027? First volume shipments of Groq 3 LPX, Nvidia's first rack-scale LPU system built on Groq's high-interactivity technology, confirmed by Nvidia. Fiscal Q3 2027 (quarter ending late October 2026) — the item's 'later this quarter' for volume shipments, with the item dated 8 October 2026.\nOPEN B-T2 Will the U.S. Department of Justice or FTC bring a formal enforcement action, or require changes to Nvidia's 2025 Groq licensing/acqui-hire transaction, by Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027? A formal DOJ/FTC challenge or settlement requiring restructuring of the $20 billion Groq transaction ($17 billion licensing agreement plus $3 billion Nvidia stock bonus pool). Nvidia's fiscal year-end 2027 (late January 2027) — set by rule as the current fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory matter, since the item gives no date for the probe or any decision.\nARTICLE 8 The demand has led to the blossoming of an industry that hyperscalers once had to themselves, including lucrative one-off arrangements and asset-light strategies.\nOver 300 neoclouds now rent GPUs to businesses, according to a review from researcher SemiAnalysis, up 55% in under 11 months.\nMarc Benioff, chief executive officer of Salesforce Inc., left, and Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., during the 2026 Dreamforce conference in San Francisco, California, US, on Tuesday, Sept. 15, 2026.\nNvidia GPUs are the most sought-after processors in AI, and they're in such demand that the chipmaker's stock climbed to yet another record this week, lifting its market cap close to $6 trillion.\nCustomers can now shop around for access to the chips at the giant clouds from Amazon, Microsoft and Google, as well as at so-called neoclouds like CoreWeave. They can also go to various online marketplaces or even buy the costly hardware directly.\nFor Nvidia, it all adds up to unrelenting growth, as management anticipates $108 billion in revenue for the October quarter, which would mark an 89% year-over-year jump.\nBut the paradox of choice can be a headache for companies needing computing power yesterday.\nWhile cloud infrastructure providers have ranked at the top of Nvidia's customer list for several years, the business is diversifying. Five clients accounted for at least 10% of Nvidia's accounts receivable in July quarter, up from three in January, according to a filing .\nIndustry research firm SemiAnalysis counted 323 Nvidia GPU providers as of September, up from 209 less than 11 months earlier.\n\"You're going to see a whole new crop of really, really exciting neoclouds with hundreds of billions of dollars backlog together,\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said at a Goldman Sachs tech conference in San Francisco last month.\nHere's a rundown of the various options for accessing GPUs, and why each might make sense:\nMany big companies spend tens of millions of dollars per year on a smorgasbord of cloud services from Amazon, Google and Microsoft. Since the 2022 launch of ChatGPT, companies have increasingly turned to the hyperscalers for GPUs so they can run generative AI workloads.\nThe top cloud providers come with a reputation advantage. If a software company relies on Amazon and Microsoft for GPUs and other capabilities, it won't need to panic about prospective customers questioning its suppliers.\nMicrosoft Azure signage on the exhibition floor during the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, on March 18, 2026.\n\"When you're talking to enterprises, your subprocessor had better be Azure,\" said Bindu Reddy, CEO of AI assistant startup Abacus, referring to Microsoft's cloud infrastructure.\nIn the past year, leading AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed to spending over $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, which controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025, according to industry researcher Gartner.\n\"Hyperscalers are in a good position to show trust to the enterprises because of their 10-plus years of full-stack capabilities,\" said Gartner analyst Hardeep Singh. But hyperscalers don't always have as many GPUs as enterprises require, he said.\nAndy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, told analysts in July that the retailer and cloud pioneer won't be able to serve all the demand it foresees this year.\n\"I believe this dynamic will also be true in 2027,\" he said.\nIf the hyperscalers were adequate, neoclouds wouldn't be proliferating.\nModal, a startup operating virtual sandboxes where AI agents work independent of main IT environments, went from running on the hyperscalers to signing up with the major neoclouds, and now it uses 25 of them, said CEO Erik Bernhardsson.\n\"You can get a few hundred GPUs or maybe a thousand, but at our scale, we needed way more GPUs,\" he said.\nThe hyperscalers themselves are also chasing the neoclouds. Google and Microsoft have started tapping CoreWeave, even as they all compete with one another.\n\"Some of the hyperscalers have approached us about taking care of customers they're worried about because they don't have the ability to service those customers when they need it,\" said Marc Boroditsky, chief revenue officer of Nebius, a Netherlands-based neocloud with operations in the U.S.\nVideo generation startup Reactor uses GPUs through Nebius and hyperscalers, said CEO Alberto Taiuti. The location of data centers matters, he said, because Reactor wants user-created videos to show up right away. Nebius delivers the specific GPUs Reactor needs, solid customer service and sufficient hardware and software, at a good price, Taiuti said.\nMichael Intrator, co-founder and CEO of CoreWeave, speaks during an interview with CNBC on the floor at the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in New York City, U.S., Feb. 27, 2026.\nThe most visible neoclouds can require some upfront payment, and chips might not come online for months, because providers raise funding based on contracts and set up the data center equipment, Bernhardsson said.\nIt would be difficult for CoreWeave to turn over 10,000 GPUs to a new customer with one day's notice, said Chen Goldberg, an executive vice president. CoreWeave's near-term capacity remains essentially sold out, CEO Mike Intrator said on the company's August earnings call.\nIf companies want GPUs immediately, they might have to go beyond the name brands. Some neoclouds aren't household names because they target specific countries, which can be viable in certain cases.\n\"Capacity right now is tight, and your relationships with your suppliers is actually one of the most closely guarded secrets for companies like ours,\" said Zhen Lu, CEO of Runpod.\nSpecialist neoclouds can offer more flexibility than the larger GPU clouds, which often require upfront payments and long-term commitments. Some sell so-called bare-metal GPUs, which give customers more control but also require them to manage more of the technical work themselves.\nCompanies using these smaller neoclouds share the same concerns: When can they get the GPUs, and at what price? Sunny Smith, co-founder and technology chief at Massed Compute, said customers are often willing to commit to capacity when they expect prices to rise.\nOracle , one of the world's largest cloud providers, is letting clients bring in their own GPUs. The software maker has more debt than Amazon or Microsoft, and its credit rating is lower so it has less flexibility to go on a GPU spending spree. But it's happy to operate the technology.\n\"As we're generally able to preserve and improve margins in the case of things like bring-your-own-hardware, the ROIC for those types of structures will be even higher,\" Oracle CFO Hilary Maxson told analysts on a June earnings call, using the acronym for return on invested capital.\nOracle hasn't disclosed the names of companies that choose this path. John DiFucci, a Guggenheim Securities analyst who recommends buying Oracle shares, said it would make sense for Advanced Micro Devices and Nvidia, the top two producers of GPUs, to bring their own to Oracle.\nInstead of buying thousands of GPUs, early-stage startups with limited capital can borrow them for hours at a time for less through clouds. And for companies with heavy-duty computing needs, Oracle's new route might be more attractive than building whole data centers. OpenAI committed to spending over $300 billion with Oracle over five years, but it hasn't mentioned anything about bringing in GPUs.\nOpenAI declined to comment.\nThe method might make sense for companies that have the capital to purchase AI chips but don't have enough power, data center space or skilled labor. Like its hyperscaler peers, Oracle works hard to secure all three in healthy quantities.\nAnother emerging option is to cut large deals with firms that have truckloads of GPUs for rent.\nSpaceX arranged to turn over excess capacity in separate deals with hyperscaler Google and open-source startup Reflection .\nIn April, SpaceX agreed to provide Cursor with GPUs and then bought the AI coding startup outright for $60 billion. And in May, SpaceX landed a deal to rent GPUs to Anthropic for $1.25 billion each month through mid-2029. That's more than what most startups can afford.\nFor SpaceX, though, the numbers work out nicely.\n\"The current economics have translated into a less than one-year payback on our new capital deployments for compute,\" Bret Johnsen, the company's finance chief, told analysts in August.\nIt's not only SpaceX. In July CNBC reported that Meta was working to form a cloud unit that could sell AI computing power.\nMeanwhile, companies continue to install GPU-filled servers in on-premises data centers the old-school way as CEOs work to balance capability with cost control.\nRevenue nearly doubled in the enterprise and small and medium business parts of hardware maker Lenovo's Infrastructure Solutions Group during the June quarter. \"We're seeing more and more enterprises now starting to say, 'How do I bring AI into my four walls?'\" said Vlad Rozanovich, a senior vice president.\nThe hourly spot price for an Nvidia B200 GPU has more than doubled since March, according to data from Ornn, a startup that maintains indexes.\nCollaboration software maker Dropbox relies on GPUs in its data centers, CEO Ashraf Alkarmi said.\n\"If we want to do a lot more, I think our supply chain connections will still be beneficial and a structural advantage,\" he said.\nEverpure , which sells data center storage hardware and software, has acquired its own GPUs, to run open-weight AI models for the company's software engineers, said CEO Charlie Giancarlo.\n\"In a very dynamic pricing environment, it's always good to have multiple sources that you can go to,\" he said.\nREAD A LIBRARY True\nOPEN A-T1 What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, compared with the more-than-doubling since March 2026 reported for the Ornn index? The end-2026 hourly spot rental rate for an Nvidia B200 GPU in the secondary/neocloud rental market, as tracked by Ornn's index, versus its level in March 2026 — a gauge of GPU scarcity, neocloud economics and hence the durability of demand for Nvidia data-center GPUs. End-December 2026 (calendar 2026, the 'this year' of this 10 October 2026 item); settled at the release of Ornn's B200 rental-price index for December 2026.\nFILE A-S3 Nvidia disclosed in a filing that five clients each accounted for at least 10% of its accounts receivable in the July quarter, up from three such clients in January — customer concentration in receivables increased over that period. ['library clean-1']\nFILE A-S5 AI labs Anthropic and OpenAI have committed over the past year to spending more than $500 billion between Amazon and Microsoft, CNBC reported, citing Gartner for the fact that the two cloud providers controlled 59% of the cloud infrastructure market in 2025. The commitment figure is a multi-year contracted amount between those parties, not a Nvidia figure and not a 2026 data-center capex number. ['library clean-5']\nFILE A-S6 Amazon CEO Andy Jassy told analysts in July 2026 that Amazon will not be able to serve all the cloud demand it foresees that year, and said he believes the same dynamic will hold in 2027 — a claim that hyperscaler capacity will remain short of demand this year and next. ['library clean-5']\nREAD B LIBRARY False\nOPEN B-T1 What will the hourly spot rental price of an Nvidia B200 GPU be at the end of calendar 2026, after the item reports it more than doubled between March and October 2026? The year-end 2026 level of the hourly spot rental price for an Nvidia B200 GPU, as tracked by Ornn's index. End of calendar 2026 (December 2026 reading); set from the item's reference to 2026 spot pricing and the year-end index reading.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"ref\":\"1:A-S2\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The guarantee is part of NVIDIA's disclosed support for an AI partner.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"1:A-S3\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The disclosed commitments directly inform the next reported supply commitment total.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"1:A\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA's margins and competition from custom AI chips\"],\"why\":\"Custom chips could weaken NVIDIA's pricing power and margins.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"1:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The large conditional guarantee can affect future cash and credit risk.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"1:B-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Large supply commitments could become costly if demand weakens.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"1:B\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA's margins and competition from custom AI chips\"],\"why\":\"Custom chips could weaken NVIDIA's pricing power and margins.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"2:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Memory prices can affect NVIDIA's input costs and margins.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"2:A-S5\",\"home\":\"unsure\",\"why\":\"The next-generation memory collaboration may relate to Rubin, but the article does not identify the GPU generation.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"2:A\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The remaining supplier developments do not establish a separate major NVIDIA matter.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"2:B\",\"missing\":[\"High-bandwidth memory prices and supply\"],\"why\":\"Memory shortages and pricing can affect NVIDIA's costs and ability to supply GPUs.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Volume shipments of NVIDIA's next platform can materially affect revenue.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:A-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Major cloud providers' spending is a significant driver of NVIDIA demand.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:A\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The article's principal product and demand matters are covered.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:B-S2\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Production progress and platform performance bear on the Rubin shipment ramp.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:B-S3\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The 2026 spending estimate directly bears on the hyperscaler spending question.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"3:B\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The library already holds the principal product and demand matters.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The timing of Rubin volume shipments matters to NVIDIA's revenue.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:A-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Completion of the sizable acquisition can affect NVIDIA's business and cash.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:A-T3\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Export restrictions affect access to a major data center market.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:A\",\"missing\":[\"Third-party AI infrastructure financing platforms\"],\"why\":\"The proposed financing platforms could affect customers' ability to buy and deploy NVIDIA systems.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B-S2\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The zero-China assumption shows the current revenue outlook under restrictions.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B-S4\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The agreement and expected closing directly concern the acquisition.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B-S5\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Production and partner deployments bear on Rubin's shipment progress.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B-S6\",\"home\":\"wrong\",\"why\":\"Third-party financing is not itself NVIDIA's disclosed guarantee or credit-support amount.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B-S7\",\"home\":\"unsure\",\"why\":\"Lender caution provides financing context, but does not establish NVIDIA's guarantee exposure.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"4:B\",\"missing\":[\"Third-party AI infrastructure financing platforms\"],\"why\":\"The library tracks NVIDIA's own support, not the proposed third-party financing programme.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"5:A\",\"missing\":[\"China data center export restrictions\",\"NVIDIA customer concentration\"],\"why\":\"Market access and dependence on two large customers can materially affect revenue.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"5:B-S4\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The guidance's zero-China assumption provides context for restricted market access.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"5:B-S6\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The earlier charge and lost market access provide context for the restrictions.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"5:B\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA customer concentration\"],\"why\":\"Two customers accounted for over a third of revenue, creating material customer risk.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:A-S2\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"NVIDIA's loss coverage and financing support bear directly on its customer guarantees.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:A-S3\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Cloud providers' funding shortfall bears on their ability to sustain planned data center spending.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:A-S5\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Rubin's expected revenue contribution bears on its commercial ramp.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:A-S6\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"A possible memory-related delay bears directly on Rubin's shipment timing.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:A\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA gross margins and memory costs\"],\"why\":\"The reported margin outlook cut and rising memory costs describe a separate profitability matter.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Customer financing guarantees can create material future exposure.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:B-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"A delay in Rubin's ramp could defer significant sales.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"6:B\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA gross margins and memory costs\",\"Cloud customer funding and data center spending\"],\"why\":\"Margin pressure and customers' ability to fund demand are material matters beyond the opened questions.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"7:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Commercializing the new inference system could contribute materially to NVIDIA's business.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"7:A\",\"missing\":[\"Legal and regulatory challenges to the Groq transaction\"],\"why\":\"The litigation and reported regulatory scrutiny could affect the transaction and commercialization.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"7:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Volume shipments test commercialization of the new inference system.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"7:B-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Regulatory action could affect a sizable transaction and NVIDIA's use of the technology.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"7:B\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The product launch and the Groq transaction's legal and regulatory uncertainty are covered.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"GPU rental prices provide a measurable signal of scarcity and demand for NVIDIA hardware.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:A-S3\",\"home\":\"wrong\",\"why\":\"Receivables concentration is customer credit exposure, not the amount of NVIDIA's guarantees.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:A-S5\",\"home\":\"wrong\",\"why\":\"The labs' multiyear cloud contracts are not the hyperscalers' 2026 data center capital expenditure.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:A-S6\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Unmet cloud demand provides context for a major hyperscaler's capacity investment.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:A\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA accounts receivable concentration\"],\"why\":\"Large customer receivables create a distinct collection and credit-risk matter.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"GPU rental prices provide a measurable signal of scarcity and demand for NVIDIA hardware.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"8:B\",\"missing\":[\"NVIDIA accounts receivable concentration\"],\"why\":\"Large customer receivables create a distinct collection and credit-risk matter.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"9:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"A potential acquisition of a company valued at $25 billion could materially affect NVIDIA.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"9:A\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The potential Reflection AI acquisition is the article's principal company matter.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"9:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Reflection AI is a separate potential acquisition from Hugging Face.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"9:B\",\"missing\":[],\"why\":\"The potential Reflection AI acquisition is covered.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:A-S1\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Release and performance provide context for Rubin's commercial rollout.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:A-S3\",\"home\":\"wrong\",\"why\":\"Supply constraints on sales growth do not state NVIDIA's purchase or supply commitment amount.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:A-S5\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Amazon's GPU purchases are evidence of a major hyperscaler's investment in computing capacity.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:A-S6\",\"home\":\"unsure\",\"why\":\"Global AI spending is broader than the five companies' 2026 data center spending.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:A\",\"missing\":[\"SpaceX's $40 billion NVIDIA chip purchase\",\"NVIDIA's 2028 revenue growth and supply constraints\"],\"why\":\"The large customer deal and constrained revenue outlook are separate material matters.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:B-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The scale of global AI investment is a major indicator of potential NVIDIA demand.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"10:B\",\"missing\":[\"Vera Rubin commercial rollout\",\"SpaceX's $40 billion NVIDIA chip purchase\",\"Amazon's NVIDIA GPU purchases\",\"NVIDIA's 2028 revenue growth and supply constraints\"],\"why\":\"The product rollout, large customer purchases and constrained growth outlook can materially affect NVIDIA revenue.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:A-T1\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"The large conditional guarantees can affect future cash and credit risk.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:A-T2\",\"in_library\":\"none\",\"worth\":\"yes\",\"why\":\"Investment commitments can constrain future cash allocation.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:A\",\"missing\":[\"Funding NVIDIA's investments and shareholder distributions\"],\"why\":\"The reported cash gap raises a separate question about the sustainability of capital allocation.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:B-S3\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The library explicitly compares NVIDIA's support commitments with its equity investments.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:B-S5\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The investment balance provides context for the equity investments named in the held question.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:B-S6\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"Investment commitments provide context for the equity investments named in the held question.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:B-S7\",\"home\":\"right\",\"why\":\"The data center guarantee directly contributes to disclosed support exposure.\"}\n{\"ref\":\"11:B\",\"missing\":[\"Funding NVIDIA's investments and shareholder distributions\"],\"why\":\"The held question does not track whether operating cash covers investments and shareholder payouts.\"}"}}
